import{_ as o,c as t,o as d,aj as i}from"./chunks/framework.Id7s26SP.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Changelog","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"changelog.md","filePath":"changelog.md"}'),n={name:"changelog.md"};function a(s,e,c,r,l,h){return d(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="changelog" tabindex="-1">Changelog <a class="header-anchor" href="#changelog" aria-label="Permalink to “Changelog”">​</a></h1><p>User-visible changes in each production release of the Primitive platform: new capabilities, additions and changes to the client APIs and the CLI, and fixes that change behavior you can observe. Internal work — refactoring, infrastructure, dependency bumps — is not listed. Entries are newest first; each date is a production release, grouped by feature area, with <strong>Breaking</strong>, <strong>Deprecated</strong> and <strong>Fixed</strong> tags where they apply. An <code>## Unreleased</code> section, when present, lists changes already merged since the last production release; it is date-stamped when that release ships.</p><h2 id="unreleased" tabindex="-1">Unreleased <a class="header-anchor" href="#unreleased" aria-label="Permalink to “Unreleased”">​</a></h2><h3 id="databases" tabindex="-1">Databases <a class="header-anchor" href="#databases" aria-label="Permalink to “Databases”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A database (<code>db3</code>) is where an app&#39;s data lives: created, typed, shared and read through <code>client.db3</code> in the JavaScript and Swift clients, <code>primitive db3</code> on the CLI and <code>ctx.db3</code> in a server function. Each database is synced to its readers&#39; devices, or has sync off and is served only by functions.</li><li>A database&#39;s models are declared once in a <code>db3-types/&lt;type&gt;.toml</code> file: <code>config push</code> generates typed models for the web, iOS and functions (the <code>Db3</code> namespace of <code>functions/primitive.d.ts</code>), installs the type in every database of it and enforces its models, indexes, unique constraints, timestamps, auto-populated fields and CEL triggers on every writer; <code>primitive db3-types list|get|push-status|push --retry</code> follow a push, <code>primitive db3 type attach</code> types an existing database, and a type file cannot be pruned while a database is of it (<code>DB3_TYPE_IN_USE</code>).</li><li>Client codegen reads the whole <code>db3-types/</code> directory (<code>js-bao-codegen-v2 -i</code>, <code>swift-bao-codegen --input</code>, and <code>inputs</code> in <code>bao-codegen.json</code>) and generates each model once; a model several types hold is declared in one file and named from the others with <code>[type] models</code>, and a model needs no <code>id</code> field.</li><li>A db3 field can take its values from a string enum declared in <code>schemas/</code> with <code>ref = &quot;&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;&lt;file&gt;.&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;schemas.&lt;file&gt;.&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code>: <code>config push</code> sends it as <code>type = &quot;string&quot;</code> with the def&#39;s <code>enum</code>, <code>config diff</code> and <code>config pull</code> read the expanded type, and <code>js-bao-codegen-v2</code> and <code>swift-bao-codegen</code> read <code>schemas/</code> beside <code>db3-types/</code> to generate the same models.</li><li>Records are read and written with the generated models on a device, <code>ctx.db3(id, type).model(name)</code> in a function (<code>ctx.db3(id)</code> for an untyped database, <code>{ asUser }</code> to act as a member), and <code>client.db3.records(id)</code> or <code>primitive db3 records</code> over HTTP: cursor-paged queries with <code>$all</code> and <code>$size</code>, counts, grouped aggregates, <code>include</code> for related records, <code>upsertOn</code>, conditional writes and <code>ifNotExists</code>, <code>increment</code> counters, string-set <code>$add</code>, <code>$remove</code> and <code>$clear</code>, atomic or per-operation batches of up to 100 operations that may read between their writes, and <code>batchRead</code> for several reads answered from one state of the database, under one set of limits (100 records a page by default and 10,000 at most, 8 MiB a page, 3 include levels); a call naming a database the app does not have, or one since deleted, is refused <code>DB3_NOT_FOUND</code>.</li><li>A synced database is subscribed with <code>client.db3.subscribe</code> (<code>available</code> once the device&#39;s copy answers queries, <code>ready</code> once it has caught up, <code>state</code> and the <code>db3:*</code> events), reads and writes offline — <code>client.db3.create</code> included, which returns as soon as the database exists on the device, online or offline, and commits it to the server in the background — queues writes, refuses on the device a write the user&#39;s permission or the type&#39;s declarations would refuse and hands back the rest on <code>db3:write-refused</code> (<code>save({ confirm: true })</code> waits for the database&#39;s answer), turns <code>gone</code> with the reason (<code>permission</code>, <code>sync-off</code>, <code>deleted</code> or <code>client-outdated</code>) when the device loses access and returns to <code>live</code> if access is restored while it is open, is closed when its user signs out or another signs in, and is removed with <code>client.db3.evict</code> or a retention policy.</li><li>A synced database lives on the device in the browser&#39;s origin-private file system on the web — set up by the Vue template with nothing to configure, or in another web app with <code>databaseConfig: { type: &quot;opfs&quot; }</code> and the worker, broker and <code>js-bao/sqlite3.wasm</code> it names — in the app&#39;s container on iOS and in a <code>node-sqlite</code> directory in Node.js, and a device that cannot keep one reports <code>FORMAT2_STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE</code>.</li><li><code>client.db3.create({ localOnly: true })</code> creates a database that lives on one device only and is never sent to the server: its writes are checked and confirmed on the device, its indexes (member indexes included) are kept there, and only <code>client.db3.delete</code> or <code>logout({ wipeLocal: true })</code> removes it.</li><li>Fields declared <code>indexed</code> or <code>unique</code> are indexed in every database of the type, and <code>client.db3.indexes</code> / <code>primitive db3 indexes</code> register and drop per-database indexes, including named unique constraints and member indexes on string-set fields (<code>members: true</code>, CLI <code>--members</code>) that keep <code>$contains</code>, any-of and facets fast on a large database; <code>on</code> (<code>&quot;both&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;server&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;device&quot;</code>) chooses where each index is built, member indexes included, and each listed index carries its <code>kind</code> and <code>on</code>.</li><li>A text index (<code>kind: &quot;text&quot;</code>, CLI <code>--kind text</code>) over a model&#39;s string fields answers a <code>$search</code> filter on the server and on each device holding the database, in the JavaScript and Swift clients, with every record ranked by its <code>_score</code>.</li><li>A <code>vector</code> field stores an embedding in the JavaScript and Swift clients, and a vector index (<code>kind: &quot;vector&quot;</code>, placed on the device) answers <code>$near</code> queries on each device holding the database, exactly or, with <code>index: { mode: &quot;ann&quot; }</code>, from an approximate index whose model one device trains and every other device fetches.</li><li>Each device builds the indexes placed on it as they are registered or dropped, without a resubscribe: <code>client.db3.indexes.local</code> lists them with their build status and <code>client.db3.indexes.skipped</code> the ones this device builds nothing for, <code>decline</code> and <code>accept</code> choose which ones this device keeps, and the <code>deviceIndexes</code> client option sets the default.</li><li><code>maxDrift</code> on a query, <code>count</code> or <code>queryOne</code> refuses an answer from a device&#39;s copy too far behind with <code>DB3_DRIFT_EXCEEDED</code>, and the database&#39;s state reports that <code>drift</code>.</li><li><code>query</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>aggregate</code> and batch writes answer what they cost the server in <code>cost.rowsRead</code> and <code>cost.rowsWritten</code>, and the <code>analytics/db3/queries</code> and <code>analytics/requests</code> reports rank those costs per query shape, endpoint and function.</li><li><code>primitive db3 records query</code>, <code>count</code> and <code>aggregate</code> take <code>--explain</code> to show an app admin or the database&#39;s owner how the server answers the read — the indexes it used and the rows it scanned — and <code>--index &lt;name&gt;</code> (or <code>none</code>) to force it onto or off an index, and in development the JavaScript client&#39;s <code>devMode: { servedIndexes: true }</code> logs which index served each read on the device.</li><li>A database download cut short by a reload, a closed tab or app, or a dropped connection continues where it stopped at the next subscribe.</li><li>A database&#39;s image blob shows directly: use its URL as an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> source on the web, and <code>BlobImage</code> in SwiftUI.</li><li><code>primitive db3 list --user-id &lt;id&gt;</code> lists every database a user can open, with a <code>VIA</code> column (<code>--via held,shared,app</code>).</li><li>Access is per database: share with users, groups (a membership in the group, or the app owner or admin role, is required), a link or the whole app (<code>setAppAccess</code>) — a share email links to the app&#39;s base URL unless <code>documentUrl</code> names a page — let members ask with <code>requestAccess</code> and owners hear each request and its resolution live (<code>db3:access-request-created</code> / <code>db3:access-request-resolved</code>), check with <code>client.db3.get</code>, <code>auth.db3(id, type).permissions()</code> or <code>validateAccess</code>, list a user&#39;s databases with <code>client.db3.listMine</code> (filtered by <code>via</code>; a sync-off database is listed to no one), cancel one email share before the invitee signs up with <code>permissions(id).revokePendingInvitation</code>, and give a user their own database under a key you choose with <code>getOrCreateWithAlias</code> or bind a key to an existing one with <code>client.db3.aliases.set</code> / <code>resolve</code> / <code>delete</code>, held by that user at <code>owner</code> or, from a function, at the <code>reader</code> or <code>read-write</code> its <code>permission</code> names.</li><li>Every database has a custodian, a member or the app: <code>primitive db3 create --custodian app</code>, <code>db3 list --custodian</code>, <code>db3 transfer</code>, <code>db3 owner-row set</code> and the sync switch (<code>db3 sync set</code>), and <code>ctx.api.db3.create</code>, <code>transfer</code>, <code>setOwnerRow</code>, <code>setSync</code> and <code>setAppAccess</code> under the <code>db3:transfer</code>, <code>db3:setOwnerRow</code>, <code>db3:setSync</code> and <code>db3:setAppAccess</code> capabilities; a departing member&#39;s databases pass to the app or are deleted, and a transfer in progress answers <code>409 TRANSFER_PENDING</code> to another transfer or owner-row change.</li><li><code>client.db3.ingests</code> and <code>primitive db3 ingest</code> bulk-load records (<code>--mode refresh</code> replaces a dataset), and <code>primitive db3 export</code> / <code>import</code> (<code>--into</code>, <code>--keep-id</code>) move a database within or between apps.</li><li>A server function acting about a member it is not lists that member&#39;s databases with <code>ctx.api.db3.listHeldByUser({ userId })</code> and <code>ctx.api.db3.listSharedWithUser({ userId })</code> — the entries <code>client.db3.listMine</code> returns for <code>via: &quot;held&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;shared&quot;</code>, narrowed by <code>tag</code> and <code>type</code>, the held listing including the member&#39;s sync-off databases — and binds, finds and removes one&#39;s alias with <code>ctx.api.db3.aliases.set</code>, <code>resolve</code> and <code>delete</code>. A filtered page can be short or empty with <code>hasMore: true</code>; follow <code>nextCursor</code>.</li><li>A <code>[[function.triggers.db3]]</code> entry runs a function once per batch of changes to any database of a type, admitted by <code>allowCallers({ db3: true })</code>.</li><li>A database&#39;s files are <code>client.db3.blobs(id)</code> on both clients and <code>ctx.api.db3.blobs.upload</code> in a function.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> The documents and databases surfaces this release&#39;s databases replace — <code>client.documents</code>, <code>client.document(id)</code>, <code>client.databases</code> and <code>client.databaseTypeConfigs</code> with their Swift twins, the document open, sync and awareness methods, <code>collections.listCollectionsForDocument</code>, <code>ctx.doc</code>, <code>ctx.db</code> and <code>ctx.api.documents</code> / <code>ctx.api.databases</code>, and <code>primitive documents</code>, <code>primitive databases</code> and <code>primitive database-type-configs</code> — stay served; each of those CLI commands, and creating a format-1 document, prints a deprecation warning, and new data belongs in a <code>db3</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="agents" tabindex="-1">Agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#agents" aria-label="Permalink to “Agents”">​</a></h3><ul><li>An agent is a prompt of <code>kind = &quot;agent&quot;</code> declared in TOML and shipped with <code>config push</code>: it names the server functions and client tools the model may call, the events members record, its turn context and its limits, with model settings on a <code>[configs.agent]</code> configuration; while an agent uses a function, archiving or deleting it, or a schema change the agent cannot take, is refused with <code>FUNCTION_USED_BY_AGENT</code>.</li><li>A server function creates a session with <code>ctx.agents.createSession</code>, and members then use it through <code>client.agents.sessions</code> in the JavaScript and Swift clients: <code>open</code> returns a live view of the conversation — a synced database with the session&#39;s id, read from the device&#39;s copy — with <code>onChange</code>, <code>send</code>, <code>answer</code>, <code>recordEvent</code>, <code>cancel</code>, <code>rename</code>, <code>addMember</code> and <code>removeMember</code> act on it, and a member removed from the session sees the view empty, its <code>gone</code> set and the copy leave the device.</li><li>A tool can require a person&#39;s approval or be answered by the member&#39;s client, pausing the turn until the first valid answer, and <code>ctx.tool.attachArtifact</code> keeps an app-only value beside the call that the model never sees, which the app reads with the session view&#39;s <code>artifact</code>.</li><li>A turn&#39;s context — a server function&#39;s return or the send&#39;s own <code>context</code>, validated against the agent&#39;s schema — renders as <code>turn.*</code> in the system prompt, with the send&#39;s timezone and language as <code>turn.timezone</code> and <code>turn.locale</code>; an agent selects what the model sees of earlier turns with a history function or <code>maxHistoryChars</code>.</li><li>App owners and admins list, inspect and delete any member&#39;s sessions with <code>primitive agent-sessions list</code>, <code>get</code> and <code>delete</code>. <code>primitive agent-sessions transcript</code>, <code>steps</code> and <code>artifacts</code> read a session&#39;s messages, turn steps and tool-call artifacts by its session ID, for its members and console admins of the app (a tenant-side app owner or admin outside the session needs a grant on its document), and <code>upload</code> and <code>download</code> move its files; the system prompt and turn contexts stay visible only through <code>get</code>, to app owners and admins.</li><li>An open session view&#39;s <code>attachments</code> uploads a file to the session by its id alone, with no document ID in app code: <code>upload()</code> resolves once the file landed or, offline, once it is queued, queued uploads retry and survive a reload or restart in both clients, <code>uploads()</code>, <code>pause()</code>, <code>resume()</code>, <code>cancel()</code>, <code>waitUntilUploaded()</code> and <code>read()</code> manage and read them, and a <code>send</code> whose <code>files</code> name a queued upload waits for it (its pending entry shows the state <code>waiting</code>) and is sent once it lands. Once the session is deleted or the member removed, its queued uploads and the messages waiting on them fail with <code>AGENT_SESSION_NOT_FOUND</code>. <code>client.agents.sessions.uploadAttachment</code>, <code>downloadAttachment</code> and <code>attachmentHeaders</code> do the same outside a view.</li><li><code>ctx.agents.createSession</code>, <code>getSession</code>, <code>listSessions</code>, <code>recordEvent</code> and <code>deleteSession</code> take <code>userId</code> and work from any kind of run, cron, webhooks, nested starts, tools and context functions included: the session is owned, read, recorded or deleted as that member, else the invoking user, and only its owner deletes it. A tool, context or history function acts only for the turn&#39;s initiator. Only a REST client is refused <code>AGENT_SESSION_ORIGIN_REFUSED</code>.</li><li>A presentation tool (<code>runs = &quot;presentation&quot;</code>) carries the structured data an agent hands the client with its final answer — result cards, follow-up chips: its call is final at once with the result <code>{ &quot;delivered&quot;: true }</code> and ends the turn with no further model round, and <code>view.presentations()</code> reads it in the JavaScript and Swift clients, typed by the agent&#39;s generated <code>&lt;Agent&gt;AgentPresentation</code>. A server tool declared with <code>endsTurn = true</code> ends the turn once it returns.</li><li>Every tool call of a turn carries <code>callNumber</code>, its number in the turn. A presentation&#39;s <code>references</code> name earlier results by that number, which the model reads with each result, and a number that names no earlier result of the turn is refused <code>AGENT_PRESENTATION_REFERENCE_INVALID</code> so the model corrects it.</li><li>Agent session list items and <code>get()</code> carry <code>hasUnread</code> and <code>lastReadAt</code> in both clients and in <code>ctx.agents</code>; <code>client.agents.sessions.markRead(sessionId)</code> (or <code>view.markRead()</code>) marks a session read for the user on every device, and <code>primitive agent-sessions list</code> shows an <code>UNREAD</code> column.</li><li><code>client.agents.sessions.watch()</code> in the JavaScript and Swift clients keeps a session list current — the unread dot, last activity, title, status and members — without polling or opening any document, and refreshes it after a reconnect; an open view&#39;s <code>info</code> follows the same changes, and <code>open(sessionId, { markRead: &quot;while-open&quot; })</code> keeps the open session read on the user&#39;s other devices. Each change is also the clients&#39; <code>agentSessionChanged</code> event.</li><li>Agent session list items carry the session&#39;s <code>status</code> as of its last listed activity, in both clients and in <code>ctx.agents.listSessions</code>.</li><li><code>view.handleClientTools</code> on an open agent session, in the JavaScript and Swift clients, runs a handler per client tool and answers each of the current user&#39;s waiting calls with the handler&#39;s result, including calls found again after a reconnect.</li><li>A turn&#39;s steps record each client tool call it waited on as a <code>client_call</code> step, timed from the call being written until its answer arrived, with <code>resolvedByUserId</code> naming the member who answered; a call that ends unanswered has its step <code>failed</code> with the code it ended with.</li></ul><h3 id="child-apps" tabindex="-1">Child apps <a class="header-anchor" href="#child-apps" aria-label="Permalink to “Child apps”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>primitive apps children create &lt;slug&gt;</code> creates a child app of the current environment&#39;s app for a branch — a copy of its settings and, unless <code>--no-secrets</code>, its secrets — pushes the tree into it and selects it as a machine-local environment that <code>config pull</code>, <code>push</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>pnpm dev</code> and the deploy scripts resolve with no other setup; <code>apps children list</code>, <code>get</code> and <code>delete</code> manage it, another parent admin attaches it with <code>primitive env use &lt;slug&gt;</code>, a child signs in with its parent environment&#39;s login so <code>primitive login</code> or <code>logout</code> in any of them acts on the whole tree, and <code>primitive apps list --all</code> includes children.</li><li>Every admin of the parent uses a child&#39;s admin and app APIs without an invitation; only the child&#39;s owner or the parent&#39;s console owner deletes it, and <code>primitive apps delete &lt;app-id&gt; --with-children</code> deletes an app&#39;s children before the app.</li><li>A child&#39;s own config var values go in <code>primitive/&lt;env&gt;/vars.&lt;slug&gt;.override.toml</code> beside <code>vars.toml</code>: with the child selected, <code>config push</code> sends <code>vars.toml</code> with the override&#39;s keys replacing or adding to it, <code>config diff</code> compares against those values, <code>config pull</code> writes the child&#39;s differing values into the override and never into <code>vars.toml</code>, and <code>config set vars</code> edits it; the parent and other children never read it. <code>apps children create --vars-file &lt;path&gt;</code> writes it before the first push, <code>get</code> shows it, <code>delete</code> removes it, and <code>primitive init</code> and the templates git-ignore <code>primitive/*/*.override.toml</code>. Functions cache the vars they read per version, so a child that adopts an override after its functions were pushed reads the new values once each function is pushed as a new version; <code>config push</code> notes when this applies.</li><li>A child is deleted after <code>--idle-days</code> (7–30, default 30) without a config push, an interactive sign-in or a person-started function invocation; its owner is emailed at least seven days before, and any such activity renews it.</li><li><code>pnpm cf-deploy --deploy-env &lt;env&gt; --primitive-env &lt;child&gt; --preview-alias &lt;name&gt;</code> in the Vue template uploads a preview version of the worker under that alias with the child&#39;s app id, prints its URL and registers the origin on the child, so its CORS and email sign-in checks accept it while the live worker is untouched.</li><li>In the console, <strong>My Apps</strong> lists only apps with no parent, and an app&#39;s page lists its child apps and opens each one.</li></ul><h3 id="server-functions" tabindex="-1">Server functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#server-functions" aria-label="Permalink to “Server functions”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A new or changed server function version must define an <code>authorize</code> method on the object form of <code>defineFunction</code>: <code>authorize</code> decides who may call it on every door — HTTP, agent, nested start, manual, webhook, cron and database change — with no owner or admin bypass, and a version without one is refused <code>FUNCTION_AUTHORIZE_REQUIRED</code>. An already-deployed version keeps its CEL <code>access</code> rule until you push a change; a version that carries both is refused <code>FUNCTION_ACCESS_AMBIGUOUS</code>, and the bare <code>defineFunction(handler)</code> form is deprecated.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A version with <code>authorize</code> closes its <code>inputSchema</code> by default (<code>additionalProperties: false</code>), and <code>config push</code> refuses a function that admits anyone and can write unless its TOML says <code>public = true</code> (<code>FUNCTION_PUBLIC_WRITE_UNDECLARED</code>); it also refuses an <code>inputSchema</code> keyword the validator does not enforce (<code>allOf</code>, <code>not</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>patternProperties</code>, <code>contains</code>, a schema-valued <code>additionalProperties</code>, tuple <code>items</code>), naming the path.</li><li><code>primitive-functions</code> exports <code>allow</code>, <code>deny</code> and <code>fail</code>, and the helpers <code>allowAnyone</code>, <code>requireSignedIn</code>, <code>requireRole</code>, <code>requireMember</code>, <code>requireCollectionRole</code>, <code>requireDocument</code>, <code>requireCollection</code>, <code>requireRecord</code>, <code>hasGroupRole</code> and <code>allowCallers</code>; <code>authorize</code> reads a read-only <code>auth</code> context, hands what it resolves to the handler as <code>ctx.auth</code>, and <code>ctx.reauthorize()</code> re-runs it mid-task against current data.</li><li>The generated declarations type each <code>ctx.api</code> call&#39;s body and answer from the platform&#39;s API spec, so <code>config push</code>&#39;s typecheck refuses a body key or an answer field the spec does not declare, and offer no field or call the platform does not serve.</li><li>A trigger fire or nested start that <code>authorize</code> refuses settles its run <code>denied</code>, a new terminal run status in both clients, the CLI and the console.</li><li><code>primitive functions authorize &lt;key&gt;</code> runs only a function&#39;s <code>authorize</code> and prints the decision, with <code>--user</code>, <code>--role</code>, <code>--trigger</code>, <code>--runtime</code> and <code>--input</code>, and <code>--cases</code> runs the checks in <code>functions/&lt;key&gt;.auth.json</code> with <code>@fixture</code> aliases from <code>auth-fixtures.json</code>; the console shows each function&#39;s Authorize card and its denied invocations, and <code>functions logs</code> and <code>functions get</code> show the decision.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>config push</code> refuses <code>mode</code> and <code>durable</code> in a function&#39;s TOML and <code>mode</code> on a <code>[[function.triggers.cron]]</code> entry, which it used to ignore with a warning: delete the lines — the caller picks the runtime with <code>invoke</code> or <code>start</code> — or let <code>config pull</code> strip them.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The client doors to a one-request workflow run, a prompt and an integration are gone: in both clients <code>workflows.runSync</code>, <code>client.prompts.execute</code> and <code>client.integrations.call</code> are removed with their member routes, and generated workflow invokers drop <code>runSync</code>. Call a server function with <code>functions.invoke</code> and use <code>ctx.prompts.run</code> or <code>ctx.integrations.call</code> inside it.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Registered database operations, database subscriptions and the clients&#39; direct database surface are removed: <code>config push</code> refuses <code>[[operations]]</code> and <code>[[subscriptions]]</code> in a database type file, and the database operation and subscription commands and client methods are gone. Read and write records with <code>ctx.db</code> in a server function and publish changes with <code>ctx.channels.publish</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The workflow step DSL, Rhai scripts and standalone webhook and cron objects are retired: <code>config push</code> refuses workflow and script blocks, and <code>primitive webhooks</code>, <code>primitive cron-triggers</code> and the JavaScript client&#39;s <code>cronTriggers</code> are removed. Move the logic into a server function with webhook and cron triggers.</li><li>Server functions import code from directories outside the config tree, such as code your web client already runs, once <code>functionSources</code> in <code>primitive/config.json</code> lists them.</li><li><code>assertTrigger(ctx, kind)</code> from <code>primitive-functions</code> refuses every trigger but <code>kind</code> and narrows <code>ctx</code> to it, so a tool function reads <code>ctx.tool</code> without a guard, and <code>FunctionContextFor&lt;kind&gt;</code> types a helper that takes the narrowed context.</li><li><code>ctx.users.forEachBatch</code> and <code>primitive users walk start</code> run a batch function over every user a filter matches, under the <code>users-batch</code> trigger that <code>allowCallers({ usersBatch })</code> admits, and <code>primitive users walk status</code> and <code>terminate</code> follow and stop a walk.</li><li><code>client.functions.listRuns</code> lists the task runs the signed-in user started, filtered by function, status or document, and <code>listRunSteps</code> lists one run&#39;s steps, in both clients.</li><li>A document <code>batch</code>, <code>ctx.api.documents.records.bulk</code> and <code>primitive documents records bulk</code> accept an <code>upsert</code> operation that addresses a record by a declared unique constraint, compound or single-field: the record holding those values gets only the supplied fields, and a missing one is created. The result&#39;s new <code>upserted</code> list gives each upsert&#39;s record <code>id</code> and whether it was <code>created</code>.</li><li>A task run that shows no progress for 30 minutes is ended <code>failed</code> with the new <code>errorCode</code> <code>FUNCTION_STALLED</code>, so polling a run never needs a timeout of its own. A step&#39;s own <code>timeout</code> and retry delay extend the wait, and sleeps, event waits and retry delays never count.</li><li>A task run&#39;s <code>slice</code> block adds <code>lastProgressAt</code>, <code>openStep</code>, <code>openStepStartedAt</code> and <code>parkedAt</code> in both clients, and <code>primitive functions runs</code> shows a <code>LAST PROGRESS</code> column.</li><li>An integration call made inside a server function&#39;s <code>step.do</code> names its step in <code>primitive integrations logs</code>: a STEP column, <code>--step &lt;name&gt;</code> to filter, and <code>correlation.stepId</code>, <code>correlation.stepOccurrence</code> and <code>detail.stepAttempt</code> under <code>--json</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>ctx.api.documents.blobs.uploadWithoutId</code> is removed from <code>primitive-functions</code>; it only ever answered 405. Upload with <code>ctx.api.documents.blobs.upload</code> and a <code>blobId</code> you choose. The server no longer serves <code>PUT /documents/{documentId}/blobs</code> without an id.</li><li>A server function lists what a named member holds, page by page: <code>ctx.api.documents.listOwnedByUser</code>, <code>ctx.api.documents.listSharedWithUser</code> and <code>ctx.api.collections.listForUser</code>, each taking a <code>userId</code>. Over REST they are <code>GET /users/{userId}/owned-documents</code>, <code>/shared-documents</code> and <code>/collections</code>, for an app owner or admin.</li><li>A <code>ctx.api</code> call about one user names it in <code>userId</code>; omitted or empty, it means the invoking user. <code>ctx.api.collections.list</code> and the <code>notifications</code> inbox and device calls take it. In a run with no caller, a call that names nobody throws <code>FUNCTION_SUBJECT_REQUIRED</code> rather than answering empty or binding a user-scoped alias to no member, and <code>documents.requestAccess</code> throws it too.</li><li>Naming a user other than the invoking user is allowed in a run with no caller, one an app owner or admin invoked (the role read at each call), or one whose function declares the <code>users:readAny</code> capability (reads) or <code>users:actAsAny</code> (acting as the member). Any other run an ordinary member invoked, and every tool, context or history run and anything it starts, throws <code>SUBJECT_USER_FORBIDDEN</code> — on the calls above, the user-scoped alias calls, and <code>collections.listForUser</code>, <code>documents.listOwnedByUser</code>, <code>documents.listSharedWithUser</code>, <code>groups.listUserMemberships</code> and <code>users.getRootDocument</code>. A named user who is not a member throws <code>SUBJECT_USER_NOT_FOUND</code>.</li><li>Over REST, <code>GET /collections</code>, the notification routes and <code>GET /agent-sessions/{sessionId}</code> and <code>/events</code> take <code>?userId=</code>: an app owner or admin names a member and gets that member&#39;s rows or view.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A member naming another user on <code>GET /agent-sessions?userId=</code> or <code>GET /users/{userId}/memberships</code> is refused with <code>code</code> <code>SUBJECT_USER_FORBIDDEN</code>, replacing <code>AGENT_SESSION_LIST_USER_FORBIDDEN</code> and an uncoded <code>Access denied</code>.</li><li><code>ctx.api.invitations</code> creates, reads, lists and deletes app invitations from a server function, at the <code>member</code> role and with the app&#39;s authority, so a function can invite an email that has no account without granting it anything.</li><li>Generated TypeScript and Swift types for function, prompt and agent schemas carry each constraint and annotation keyword — <code>minLength</code>, <code>maximum</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>default</code> and the rest — as a doc comment on the member or type it describes, so an editor hover shows the bounds the platform checks.</li><li>A function schema reuses a shape from its own <code>$defs</code> with <code>&quot;$ref&quot; = &quot;#/$defs/&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code>, or one declared in a <code>schemas/</code> file by <code>&quot;$ref&quot; = &quot;schemas:&lt;file&gt;#/$defs/&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code>, <code>ref = &quot;schemas.&lt;file&gt;.&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code> or the short <code>ref = &quot;&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code> and <code>ref = &quot;&lt;file&gt;.&lt;Name&gt;&quot;</code>, and push inlines the shape&#39;s definition and warns about a <code>$ref</code> it leaves unresolved; a <code>schemas/</code> def uses another file&#39;s the same way, <code>primitive schemas list</code> and <code>primitive schemas get</code> read the pushed files, each pushed function version records which defs it inlined, and <code>config diff</code> names a function pinned to a def that has since changed, been removed or been deleted.</li><li>A function schema can be written as a field table in the db3 field grammar, under <code>[function.input]</code> and <code>[function.output]</code>: <code>fields</code> for an object, <code>items</code>, <code>item.fields</code> and <code>open</code>, the bounds <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>min_length</code>, <code>max_length</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>min_items</code> and <code>max_items</code>, <code>nullable</code>, <code>coerce</code>, <code>ref</code> and <code>description</code>, and <code>schema = { ... }</code> for anything else. Push compiles it to the JSON Schema it describes and stores the same schema the equivalent <code>inputSchema</code> stores, refuses a key the table does not take (such as a db3 storage key like <code>indexed</code>) naming the dotted path and the fix, and warns about a field-table key written under <code>inputSchema</code>.</li><li>A <code>schemas/</code> def whose root carries a field-table key (<code>fields</code>, <code>item</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>min_length</code>, <code>max_length</code>, <code>min_items</code>, <code>max_items</code>, or a string <code>items</code>) is read as a field table, so a def that used one of those keys as an annotation gains the constraint on its next push, and push refuses a def that mixes them with <code>properties</code>, <code>additionalProperties</code>, a camelCase constraint such as <code>minimum</code>, <code>$ref</code>, <code>$defs</code>, <code>oneOf</code>, <code>anyOf</code> or <code>const</code>. Annotations such as <code>title</code>, <code>format</code>, <code>readOnly</code> and <code>writeOnly</code> beside them are kept; any other key in such a def is checked as a field table.</li><li>A function, prompt or agent schema uses a model of a db3 type by <code>ref = &quot;&lt;type&gt;.&lt;model&gt;&quot;</code>, <code>ref = &quot;db3.&lt;type&gt;.&lt;model&gt;&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;$ref&quot; = &quot;db3:&lt;type&gt;#/$defs/&lt;model&gt;&quot;</code>: an <code>outputSchema</code> gets the stored row (the declared fields, <code>id</code> and the type&#39;s timestamps and auto-populated fields) and every other schema the declared fields, <code>#row</code> or <code>#declared</code> chooses, validation messages and generated hovers name the projection, <code>config push</code> pushes a db3 type before the files that use it, and <code>config diff</code> names a schema pinned to a model that has since changed, been removed or been deleted.</li><li><code>config diff</code> previews the schemas a push changes: under each function, prompt and <code>schemas/</code> file it lists each schema&#39;s changes classed <code>additive</code>, <code>tightening</code> or <code>breaking</code>, the refs the schema resolves (a db3 ref with its projection) and the generated types that change, and under a shared def, db3 type, function or prompt the objects pinned to it, including ones only the server holds; <code>--json</code> rows carry them as <code>schemas</code> and <code>dependants</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Pushing a server function or a <code>schemas/</code> file needs <code>primitive-admin</code> 1.2.0 or newer: the server refuses a push from an older CLI with <code>CLI_VERSION_TOO_OLD</code>, and one without the compiled schemas a current CLI sends with <code>COMPILED_PAYLOAD_REQUIRED</code>, each naming the version to install. Run <code>npm install -g primitive-admin@latest</code> and push again.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Generated types are one <code>functions/primitive.d.ts</code>, each <code>schemas/</code> def, function, prompt, db3 model and user property type in its namespace (<code>Schemas</code>, <code>Functions</code>, <code>Prompts</code>, <code>Db3</code>, <code>UserProperties</code>): <code>config push</code>, <code>config pull</code> and <code>primitive functions codegen</code> remove <code>primitive-function-types.d.ts</code>, <code>primitive-prompt-types.d.ts</code>, <code>primitive-db3-types.d.ts</code> and <code>primitive-user-properties.d.ts</code>, printing a line for each, and push refuses a function source that still imports one; write <code>import type { Functions, Schemas } from &quot;./primitive&quot;</code> and <code>Functions.&lt;Key&gt;Input</code>; a top-level <code>Primitive*</code> name keeps its spelling.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Generated types follow the compiled schemas: a field that refs a shared def or a db3 model is a reference to its one declaration (<code>Schemas.Money</code>, <code>Db3.TasksRow</code>) instead of <code>unknown</code>, and <code>const</code>, <code>anyOf</code> and <code>nullable</code> shape the type (<code>&quot;quote&quot;</code>, <code>A | B</code>, <code>T | null</code> in TypeScript; a one-case enum, <code>JSONValue</code> and <code>T?</code> in Swift) instead of a doc comment.</li><li>Generated TypeScript invokers, agent client files and user property types export each enum&#39;s values as <code>&lt;Type&gt;&lt;Field&gt;Values = [...] as const</code>, and <code>functions codegen --lang swift</code> writes <code>Primitive.generated.swift</code>, declaring each <code>schemas/</code> def, prompt output type and referenced db3 row once for the per-key files to reference.</li><li><code>functions/primitive.d.ts</code> declares a <code>Db3.&lt;Model&gt;Filter</code> and a <code>Db3.&lt;Model&gt;Sort</code> for each db3 model: annotate a <code>ctx.db3</code> filter or sort with them, and <code>config push</code>&#39;s typecheck refuses an operator or a value that a declared field&#39;s kind doesn&#39;t take, such as <code>$startsWith</code> on a number. A field the model doesn&#39;t declare takes any operator. The <code>ctx.db3</code> and <code>auth.db3</code> model handles type the filters and sorts of <code>query</code>, <code>count</code> and <code>aggregate</code> the same way: a mismatch there is a <code>config push</code> warning in this release and the function still ships. Add a <code>functions/</code> declaration file filling <code>Db3FilterTyping</code> to make it an error now.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The production <code>ctx.api</code> and the <code>primitive-functions</code> types leave out the experimental methods, which production does not serve: <code>ctx.api.resourceMetadata</code> and the notifications inbox (<code>notifications.list</code>, <code>unreadCount</code>, <code>markRead</code>, <code>markAllRead</code> and <code>send</code>); the push-device methods stay. A function that calls them works only outside production; type it against <code>primitive-functions@alpha</code>, which keeps them.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A webhook delivery signed with a newly created <code>{{secrets.KEY}}</code> signing secret verifies right away, instead of being refused with <code>401</code> for up to a minute.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>ctx.api.documents.blobs.upload</code> stores the media type a server function names in <code>&quot;Content-Type&quot;</code>, instead of always <code>application/octet-stream</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive functions codegen --lang swift</code> leaves unchanged output files untouched, so an Xcode build no longer recompiles the whole app every time.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Swift types generated from a function or agent schema now render three shapes the TypeScript types already did: an <code>enum</code> that repeats a value gets one case per value, where the generated Swift used to fail to compile, and an <code>enum</code> or a <code>type</code> list beside a <code>oneOf</code> that is not a valid tagged union renders as that enum or type, where <code>--lang swift</code> used to refuse the schema.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>invoke</code> and <code>start</code> refuse an input that breaks a declared <code>minLength</code>, <code>maxLength</code>, <code>maxItems</code>, <code>pattern</code> or closed-object rule with <code>400 FUNCTION_INPUT_INVALID</code> before the handler runs.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A task run the platform could not begin settles <code>failed</code> with <code>errorCode</code> <code>ENGINE_START_FAILED</code> and no step run, instead of <code>FUNCTION_THREW</code>; the start retries for about a second before answering <code>500 INSTANCE_CREATE_FAILED</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="large-documents" tabindex="-1">Large documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#large-documents" aria-label="Permalink to “Large documents”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Large documents are record-log documents (<code>documentFormat: 3</code>) and the earlier large-document format is no longer served: creating or opening a <code>documentFormat: 2</code> document fails with <code>DOCUMENT_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED</code>, and <code>--large</code> creates the new format; load an earlier export into a new large document with <code>primitive documents ingest</code>.</li><li>A large document is created with <code>documentFormat: 3</code> (CLI: <code>primitive documents create --format 3</code>, or <code>--large</code>) and opens through the same document, model and query APIs in the JavaScript and Swift clients, and a write the platform cannot keep comes back on the <code>document:write-refused</code> event with every withdrawn write.</li><li>An app bulk loads records into a large document from its own code with <code>documents.ingests</code> in the JavaScript and Swift clients, and <code>primitive documents ingests</code> lists, inspects and aborts a document&#39;s load sessions.</li><li>A large document&#39;s snapshot reuses the chunks that did not change, so a client that holds an earlier one downloads only the rest.</li><li><code>primitive documents inspect</code> reports a large document&#39;s write progress, writers and bulk load, and <code>--refused</code> lists the rows a load refused; <code>primitive documents ingest</code> takes <code>--mode refresh</code> to delete the records a load does not name.</li><li>In Node.js, a large document stays on disk across restarts with <code>databaseConfig: { type: &quot;node-sqlite&quot;, options: { recordLogDirectory } }</code>, and a document&#39;s <code>GET /documents/{documentId}/schema</code> adds an <code>observed</code> section with the kinds each undeclared field has held.</li><li>A large document&#39;s fields hold a string, a number, a boolean, <code>null</code> or an array of strings, on every write path: an array of strings is a string set wherever it is written (membership queries find it; an array replaces its members, <code>{ $add, $remove, $clear }</code> from record routes and server functions change them, <code>null</code> empties it), a field keeps the string-set or scalar kind of its first non-null value, a model takes at most 1,000 field names, and field and model names are identifiers. A write outside these rules — an object, an array of anything but strings, the other kind, the 1,001st field name — is refused with <code>DOCUMENT_ENTRY_INVALID</code>, naming the model and field, and a bulk load&#39;s refused row carries the reason, which <code>documents inspect --refused</code> prints.</li></ul><h3 id="documents" tabindex="-1">Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#documents" aria-label="Permalink to “Documents”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>documents.validateAccess</code> on a document the server does not hold — deleted, never existed, or in another app — answers <code>404 NOT_FOUND</code> in both clients instead of <code>DOCUMENT_DELETED</code>; on a document whose create has not yet reached the server it waits for the commit and answers <code>owner</code>, failing with <code>PENDING_CREATE</code> when the device is offline or the create has not landed within 30 seconds.</li><li>A document record query, <code>ctx.doc(id).model(name).query</code> in a server function or <code>primitive documents records query</code>, takes a <code>sort</code> (<code>--sort</code>) and answers 100 records by default and up to 10,000 on request, refusing a larger <code>limit</code> with <code>400</code> instead of clamping it.</li><li>An app admin, in-app or console, can delete any document of the app directly with <code>documents.delete</code>, as an app owner already could.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A compound unique constraint holds across writers: a record saved by a server function, a workflow, the REST API or the CLI now blocks a client save of the same values, and the reverse.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>me.sharedDocuments()</code> no longer skips documents when paging with a small <code>limit</code>. A page can now be short or empty while <code>hasMore</code> is <code>true</code>; keep following <code>nextCursor</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The dev tools&#39; Document and Blob explorers and the Swift <code>PrimitiveAppState.fetchDocuments()</code> list every owned and shared document, not only the first page of each, and <code>fetchDocuments()</code> lists the locally cached documents while the server&#39;s page is pending, replacing them when it arrives.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A document listing fetched for a user who has since signed out is dropped instead of being kept for the next signed-in user, in both clients.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A save from a server function, the REST API or the CLI no longer duplicates a unique value held by a record the constraint&#39;s index has no entry for (one written before the constraint existed, or imported); the save is refused with the unique-constraint error, so a connected JavaScript client no longer drops the original record.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In the Swift client, an <code>openDocument</code> that waits on the network always resolves or throws <code>.networkTimeout</code> within <code>availabilityWait</code>, and <code>setLogLevel(.debug)</code> traces each open.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In the Swift client, documents cached on a device for one user, pending creates included, no longer load for another user who signs in on the same device.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In the Swift client, <code>logout(wipeLocal: true)</code> deletes only the signed-out user&#39;s local documents, instead of every user&#39;s on the device, another user&#39;s unsynced creates included.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Two documents that each hold a record with the same id keep their own records in the client&#39;s local query tables, so a <code>count()</code> or <code>query()</code> scoped to one of them no longer comes back short; a local store written by an earlier release is rebuilt on its next open and keeps every row.</li></ul><h3 id="models-and-queries" tabindex="-1">Models and queries <a class="header-anchor" href="#models-and-queries" aria-label="Permalink to “Models and queries”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A <code>stringset</code> field&#39;s values are returned sorted by Unicode code point, each value once, from every surface — model reads, queries, server reads, the CLI and the Swift client, in documents, large documents and databases — rather than in the order they were added; sort a copy to display another order.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>aggregate</code> answers an array of rows instead of an object keyed by group value: one row per group, <code>{ group: { &lt;field&gt;: &lt;value&gt; }, count, &lt;type&gt;_&lt;field&gt; }</code>, on the model statics in both clients, document and database aggregates, server functions, the Swift <code>DoDb.aggregate</code> and the CLI. Every operation keeps its key even when it is the only one, group values keep their type, an ungrouped server aggregate is one row with an empty <code>group</code>, and two <code>groupBy</code> entries that would share a key throw <code>Ambiguous aggregation group keys</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> In a server function, <code>ctx.doc(id).model(name).aggregate</code> resolves to the rows themselves; HTTP, the CLI&#39;s <code>--json</code> and <code>ctx.db(...).model(...).aggregate</code> keep the <code>{ result }</code> envelope around them.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A model field cannot take a name a generated model already uses on either client, such as <code>syncState</code>, <code>save</code> or <code>related</code> (the models guide lists them): codegen refuses a schema that declares one, and a record write carrying one fails with <code>RESERVED_FIELD_NAME</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>createModelClass</code> is removed from <code>js-bao</code> and the JavaScript client: define a model as a class that extends <code>BaseModel</code> and attach its schema with <code>defineModelSchema</code> and <code>attachAndRegisterModel</code>.</li><li><code>aggregate</code> groups a <code>date</code> field by calendar <code>month</code>, <code>quarter</code> or <code>year</code> with a <code>{ field, period }</code> <code>groupBy</code> entry, keyed <code>&lt;field&gt;_&lt;period&gt;</code> in each row&#39;s <code>group</code>, on the model statics in both clients and on a document&#39;s model in a server function.</li><li>The JavaScript client&#39;s <code>registerModelTimeoutMs</code> option sets how long <code>client.registerModel</code> waits for databases still connecting before it rejects <code>REGISTER_MODEL_TIMEOUT</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A query whose projection names a <code>stringset</code> field returns the field&#39;s values instead of an empty set.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A membership <code>groupBy</code> (<code>{ field, contains }</code>) on the JavaScript model statics answers one complete row per value, where it used to answer a group per record when more than one record carried the member.</li></ul><h3 id="collections" tabindex="-1">Collections <a class="header-anchor" href="#collections" aria-label="Permalink to “Collections”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Collections have fixed roles — <code>owner</code>, <code>manager</code>, <code>editor</code>, <code>contributor</code> and <code>viewer</code> — held by users and groups as principals: collection type configs are gone (<code>config push</code> refuses a <code>collection-type-configs/</code> file), and <code>collectionType</code>, <code>permission</code> on members, <code>getAccess</code>, <code>grantGroupPermission</code> and <code>revokeGroupPermission</code> are deprecated in both clients in favour of <code>role</code>, <code>listMembers</code>, <code>addGroupMember</code>, <code>setMemberRole</code> and <code>removeMember</code>; existing collections were migrated to roles.</li><li>A collection has a custodian who holds <code>owner</code> and cannot leave until it is transferred: a server function or the admin API names one with <code>custodian</code> (<code>app</code>, or a member) on <code>collections.create</code>, and <code>collections.transfer</code>, <code>primitive collections transfer</code>, <code>collections list --custodian</code>, <code>resolveRole</code> and <code>settings.memberList</code> in both clients, the CLI and server functions, and <code>requireCollectionRole</code> in a function&#39;s <code>authorize</code>, work with it. Collections and groups a removed member held pass to the app when anyone else holds a role on them and are deleted otherwise, and a transfer in progress answers <code>409 TRANSFER_PENDING</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Collection <code>contextId</code> is removed from the API, rules, both clients and the CLI; bind a collection to an outside entity with <code>initialMetadata</code> on create and read it in rules as <code>md.self.&lt;category&gt;.&lt;key&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Collection names are labels, not unique within an app, so creating or renaming a collection never answers a name <code>409</code>, and <code>primitive collections import</code> refuses a name that more than one target collection carries.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A collection&#39;s <code>documentCount</code> stays accurate when documents are added to or removed from it at the same time.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Deleting or unsharing a collection shared with a group, removing a document from it, and revoking a document&#39;s group grant no longer fail with <code>403</code> on older grants.</li></ul><h3 id="users-and-groups" tabindex="-1">Users and groups <a class="header-anchor" href="#users-and-groups" aria-label="Permalink to “Users and groups”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Groups have fixed roles — <code>owner</code>, <code>manager</code> and <code>member</code> — and are addressed by <code>groupId</code> alone: group type configs and their rule sets are gone (<code>config push</code> refuses a <code>group-type-configs/</code> file), the <code>(groupType, groupId)</code> forms of the group methods in both clients, <code>groupType</code> on create and grants, and <code>isMemberOf(groupType, groupId)</code> / <code>memberGroups(groupType)</code> in rules are deprecated in favour of the one-argument forms, and existing groups were migrated to roles with their creators as owners.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A client session can no longer create a group (<code>403 FUNCTION_ROUTE_FUNCTION_ONLY</code>): create one from a server function with <code>ctx.api.groups.create</code> or from the admin API (<code>primitive groups create --owner &lt;user-id&gt;</code>, or the console), naming a custodian and initial members; the server assigns every group&#39;s id and refuses a create that names one.</li><li><code>primitive groups members add</code> takes an existing app user&#39;s email, and <code>primitive waitlist bulk-invite --ids</code> invites the entries you name.</li><li>Every group has a custodian — the member holding the <code>owner</code> row, or the app — and <code>settings.memberList</code>; <code>addMember</code> takes a <code>role</code>, <code>setMemberRole</code> changes it, <code>groups.transfer</code> (<code>primitive groups transfer</code>) hands the group to another member, a function lists a custodian&#39;s groups with <code>listByCustodian</code> and gates on a role with <code>hasGroupRole(groupId, role)</code>, and granting a group a database permission or a collection role needs a membership in that group or the app owner or admin role (<code>GROUP_MEMBERSHIP_REQUIRED</code>).</li><li>The console&#39;s Groups pages work by id, with a Custodian column, member roles, an owner-row control and a transfer dialog, and Manage User shows a member&#39;s holdings.</li><li><code>groups.create</code> accepts <code>initialMetadata</code> from <code>ctx.api.groups.create</code> and <code>primitive groups create --initial-metadata</code>.</li><li>An app owner or admin sets a user&#39;s display name and avatar, at creation or later: <code>primitive users create --name --avatar-url</code>, <code>primitive users set-profile &lt;user-id&gt; --name|--avatar-url|--clear-avatar</code>, <code>ctx.api.users.setProfile</code> from a server function, and <code>name</code>/<code>avatarUrl</code> when adding a user by email. An avatar URL written this way or by <code>me.update</code> must be an <code>http:</code> or <code>https:</code> URL; any other scheme is refused with <code>400</code> and nothing is written.</li><li>User listings mark each <code>+primitivetest</code> test user with <code>testUser</code>, and <code>primitive users list --test-users exclude|only</code>, <code>testUsers</code> on <code>GET /users</code> and <code>ctx.api.users.list</code>, and the console&#39;s Users page filter them out or show only them.</li><li><code>ctx.users.query</code>, <code>count</code> and <code>aggregate</code> in a server function, the admin API and <code>primitive users query</code>, <code>count</code> and <code>aggregate</code> search an app&#39;s users by their base fields and declared properties (<code>properties.&lt;name&gt;</code>), and <code>primitive users index</code> shows the user index&#39;s status, rebuilds it and reconciles it.</li><li><code>user-properties.toml</code> declares an app&#39;s typed user properties: <code>config push</code> publishes the schema, <code>primitive user-properties get</code> shows the active one, and codegen types them as <code>UserProperties</code> in server functions and both clients.</li><li>A server function reads and writes user properties with <code>ctx.users.get</code>, <code>setProperties</code>, <code>addToSet</code> and <code>removeFromSet</code>, and finds the user holding a unique value with <code>ctx.users.getBy</code> or <code>primitive users get-by</code>.</li><li>A property&#39;s <code>client</code> setting lets signed-in clients read it on <code>me.get</code> and the user lookups, and lets users write their own with <code>me.update({ properties })</code>, in both clients.</li><li><code>initialProperties</code> on an invitation, <code>primitive users invite --initial-properties</code> or <code>primitive users create --initial-properties</code> gives a new user their properties at signup, and <code>ctx.invitations.getBy</code> finds a pending invitation by a unique value.</li><li><code>primitive metadata report user</code> reports which user metadata categories still hold rows on an app&#39;s current members, with their counts.</li><li><code>revokePendingInvitation</code> on groups and collections, in both clients and <code>ctx.api</code>, cancels one email share before the invitee signs up, keyed by the <code>invitationId</code> from <code>listPendingInvitations</code>, and leaves the invitation&#39;s other grants in place.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> <code>invitations.listDeferredGrants</code> and <code>revokeDeferredGrant</code> in both clients, and the deferred-grants routes they call; list and revoke a pending invitation on its database, group or collection.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> An invitation for an email that already belongs to a member, or already has a pending invitation, is refused with <code>400 USER_ALREADY_MEMBER</code> or <code>INVITATION_PENDING</code> in any letter case, instead of creating a second invitation.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The admin API&#39;s app users list (<code>GET /admin/api/apps/{appId}/users</code>) returns every member of an app, instead of at most the first 100.</li></ul><h3 id="analytics" tabindex="-1">Analytics <a class="header-anchor" href="#analytics" aria-label="Permalink to “Analytics”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>primitive analytics</code> filters events with a repeatable <code>--where &lt;field&gt;&lt;op&gt;&lt;value&gt;</code>, takes <code>--fresh</code> to bypass the short-lived answer cache, and reads activity with no signed-in user with <code>user-detail --anonymous</code>.</li><li>Analytics leaves <code>+primitivetest</code> test users out of active-user counts, cohort retention and top users by default, keeps them in the event feed, and lets <code>--test-users exclude|include|only</code> on <code>primitive analytics</code>, <code>testUsers</code> on the REST queries and <code>ctx.api.analytics</code>, and the console&#39;s Test users control choose.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A burst of tracked events is stored whole instead of losing every event past the first 250, and events that still cannot be stored are counted in an <code>analytics_relay</code> error group (<code>primitive analytics errors-groups --source analytics_relay</code>).</li></ul><h3 id="locks" tabindex="-1">Locks <a class="header-anchor" href="#locks" aria-label="Permalink to “Locks”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Locks belong to server functions only: <code>client.locks</code> and its types are removed from the JavaScript and Swift clients, the <code>lock</code> rule set is retired (<code>config push</code> refuses it), and the acquire, renew and release routes refuse any caller but a function with <code>403 FUNCTION_ROUTE_FUNCTION_ONLY</code>.</li><li>A server function waits for a named lock with <code>ctx.locks.acquire</code>, or runs code under it with <code>ctx.locks.withLock</code>, and an app admin inspects and frees the app&#39;s locks with <code>primitive locks list</code>, <code>status</code> and <code>force-release</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="prompts" tabindex="-1">Prompts <a class="header-anchor" href="#prompts" aria-label="Permalink to “Prompts”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> <code>accessRule</code> on a prompt or an integration is ignored and will be removed in the first breaking release after 2026-12-15: the calling function&#39;s <code>authorize</code> (and, for an integration, its <code>integration:&lt;key&gt;</code> capability) decides who may run it; <code>config push</code> warns once per file that still carries it, and <code>config pull</code> rewrites the file without it.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>config push</code> refuses a prompt schema keyword a Gemini configuration cannot carry without loss, such as <code>exclusiveMinimum</code> or <code>uniqueItems</code>, or a free-form object (<code>PROMPT_SCHEMA_NOT_TRANSLATABLE</code>), and one the platform does not enforce, such as <code>allOf</code> (<code>PROMPT_SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED_KEYWORD</code>), naming the path and the fix, before it sends anything, and <code>config diff</code> reports the file as invalid; the admin API&#39;s prompt and configuration writes refuse it with <code>400</code> and every refusal under <code>details.refusals</code>, as do a switch to Gemini, a restore and a duplicate that would send such a schema to Gemini.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A Gemini prompt run sends its <code>outputSchema</code> to the model with its meaning kept — unions, nullable types and constants included — and a stored schema with a keyword Gemini cannot carry fails before any model request with <code>errorCode</code> <code>PROMPT_SCHEMA_NOT_TRANSLATABLE</code>, from <code>ctx.prompts.run</code>, <code>primitive prompts execute</code>, prompt tests and evaluators.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>ctx.prompts.run</code> validates a prompt&#39;s answer against every keyword of its <code>outputSchema</code>, so a value outside <code>minLength</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>maximum</code> or another constraint fails with <code>PROMPT_OUTPUT_SCHEMA_VIOLATION</code>.</li><li>A prompt&#39;s <code>inputSchema</code> and <code>outputSchema</code>, a configuration&#39;s <code>outputSchema</code> and an agent&#39;s tool, event and turn-context schemas use shapes from their own <code>$defs</code>, <code>schemas/</code> files, other prompts&#39; schemas (<code>ref = &quot;prompt.&lt;key&gt;.output&quot;</code>, <code>prompt.&lt;key&gt;.tools.&lt;name&gt;.input</code>, <code>prompt.&lt;key&gt;.events.&lt;kind&gt;</code>) and functions&#39; (<code>fn.&lt;key&gt;.input</code>), and push inlines them, pushing each prompt after what it refs; <code>config push --only prompt/&lt;key&gt;</code> also pushes the prompts and <code>schemas/</code> files the selection refs, <code>config pull</code> writes the refs back, a prompt written from the console or the admin API is compiled the same way, and <code>config diff</code> names a prompt pinned to a shape that has since changed or been deleted.</li><li>A prompt&#39;s schemas can be written as field tables too: <code>[prompt.input]</code>, <code>[prompt.output]</code> and <code>[configs.chat.output]</code>, an agent tool&#39;s <code>input</code> and <code>output</code>, and the field-table keys directly on an agent event or the turn context, in place of <code>schema</code>. <code>config pull</code> writes a prompt&#39;s schemas back as JSON Schema.</li><li>A prompt names the prompt that judges its test cases by default with <code>defaultEvaluatorPromptKey</code> in <code>[prompt]</code>, <code>primitive prompts execute --attach &lt;path&gt;</code> sends files with a run, and <code>primitive prompts</code> and <code>primitive integrations</code> <code>tests runs list</code> page with <code>--cursor</code> while <code>tests runs get</code> reads one run.</li><li><code>primitive integrations test</code> sends headers, url-encoded forms, multipart fields and files, or a raw body (<code>--header</code>, <code>--form</code>, <code>--field</code>, <code>--file</code>, <code>--raw-body</code>, <code>--content-type</code>).</li><li>A test case on an agent prompt runs one model round from a conversation it writes in a <code>[test.agent]</code> block — user and assistant messages, tool calls with their results, and events — with the round&#39;s turn context, timezone and locale. <code>expectedToolCall</code> and <code>expectedNoToolCall</code> check the calls the model made, the text checks and an evaluator work as for a chat prompt, and every run records a <code>Round completed</code> check with the calls. A case that cannot be a round of its agent is refused with <code>AGENT_TEST_CASE_INVALID</code>.</li><li>A prompt run whose answer stopped at the configuration&#39;s <code>maxTokens</code> carries <code>truncated: true</code>, on success and on failure, from <code>ctx.prompts.run</code> and <code>primitive prompts execute</code>. A cut-off JSON answer, or a completion that used the whole limit and returned nothing, fails with the new <code>errorCode</code> <code>PROMPT_OUTPUT_TRUNCATED</code> instead of <code>PROMPT_OUTPUT_NOT_JSON</code> or <code>PROMPT_OUTPUT_SCHEMA_VIOLATION</code>; raise <code>maxTokens</code>. <code>primitive prompts execute</code> prints a warning, and a prompt test run cut off at the limit fails its <code>Output complete</code> check.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>config push</code> refuses a prompt <code>[[configs]]</code> entry that writes chat keys at its root or marks the live config with <code>isActive</code>, so move those keys under <code>[configs.chat]</code> and write <code>active = true</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> OpenRouter chat configurations now send <code>maxTokens</code> to the provider, so a configuration that sets it is capped at that many output tokens.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>config push</code> no longer refuses a reasoning setting because of which values a particular model supports; the provider decides. A value the model refuses fails the run with the provider&#39;s message, <code>upstreamStatus: 400</code> and the new <code>errorCode</code> <code>PROMPT_UPSTREAM_REJECTED</code>, from <code>ctx.prompts.run</code> and <code>primitive prompts execute</code>. A provider <code>400</code> on any prompt run carries that code.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A prompt run with <code>strictOutput = true</code> and a <code>modelOverride</code> that does not support strict structured outputs fails before any model request, as <code>config push</code> already refuses for the configured model.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive prompts execute</code>, the console&#39;s prompt test and the evaluator use a system prompt or user template larger than 25 KB (stored offloaded) instead of running without it.</li></ul><h3 id="sign-in-and-sessions" tabindex="-1">Sign-in and sessions <a class="header-anchor" href="#sign-in-and-sessions" aria-label="Permalink to “Sign-in and sessions”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>magicLinkRequest</code>, <code>otpRequest</code> and their routes are removed, and the auth and app config report only <code>emailSignInEnabled</code>; start email sign-in with <code>emailSignInRequest</code>.</li><li>An emailed sign-in link opens the page that asked for it: <code>emailSignInRequest</code> links to the page&#39;s origin by default, <code>redirectUri: null</code> asks for a code only, and <code>handleSignInCallback</code> on the JavaScript client and <code>auth.handleSignInCallback</code> on the Swift client finish a sign-in from its callback URL, emailed link and OAuth redirect alike.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>emailSignInRequest</code> with no <code>redirectUri</code> and no <code>oauthRedirectUri</code> set links to the page&#39;s origin plus <code>/oauth/callback</code> by default, where it used to send a code-only email: add that URL to the app&#39;s <code>[auth].emailRedirectUris</code> and run <code>config push</code>, or pass <code>redirectUri: null</code> to keep asking for a code only. A call that already passes a <code>redirectUri</code>, or a client with <code>oauthRedirectUri</code> set, is unaffected.</li><li>A Node script, cron job or CI step signs the JavaScript client in as a user with no browser or password by passing an app access token from <code>primitive tokens create</code> as <code>token</code>.</li><li><code>GET /app/{appId}/api/settings/sign-in</code> reads an app&#39;s public sign-in settings with no session, and <code>getAuthConfig()</code>, <code>getAppConfig()</code>, <code>checkOAuthAvailable()</code> and Google sign-in in both clients read them there.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> <code>GET /app/{appId}/api/oauth-config</code>; read <code>settings/sign-in</code>, which answers the same body.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Clearing an app&#39;s <code>googleClients</code> or <code>passkeyRpConfig</code> from the console no longer brings back Google client or passkey values set before those maps existed.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The plain-text part of an email, the sign-in link included, carries its links as written instead of HTML-escaped (<code>&amp;amp;</code>), in built-in templates, template overrides and <code>ctx.api.email.send</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="api-and-clients" tabindex="-1">API and clients <a class="header-anchor" href="#api-and-clients" aria-label="Permalink to “API and clients”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The JavaScript client loads Yjs and y-indexeddb the first time it opens a document with Yjs state, so an app that only uses databases never loads them: keep <code>yjs</code> installed as a peer dependency, <code>import &quot;yjs&quot;;</code> once to keep it in the startup bundle, and in a browser <code>await loadYjs()</code> from <code>js-bao</code> before calling js-bao&#39;s <code>Y.Doc</code> helpers, which throw <code>YjsNotLoadedError</code> until Yjs is loaded.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The database CEL context, deprecated in the last release, is removed: <code>getCelContext</code>/<code>updateCelContext</code> and the database <code>metadata</code> methods in both clients, <code>primitive databases cel-context</code> and <code>databases create --cel-context</code>/<code>--metadata</code>, the <code>databases/{databaseId}/metadata</code> routes, and <code>celContext</code>/<code>metadata</code> on database responses. A rule that still references <code>database.celContext</code> or <code>$database.metadata</code> is refused when saved.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> List responses drop the deprecated <code>cursor</code> alias and the <code>grants</code>, <code>admins</code>, <code>apps</code>, <code>documents</code> and <code>databases</code> array keys, so read rows from <code>items</code> and page with <code>nextCursor</code>; the Swift client&#39;s <code>DeferredGrantListResult</code> drops its <code>grants</code> property and <code>init(grants:)</code> initializer the same way — read <code>items</code> and build pages with <code>init(items:nextCursor:hasMore:)</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The deprecated <code>admin-data/*</code> database record routes and <code>ctx.api.databases.adminData</code> in <code>primitive-functions</code> are removed; use the <code>records/*</code> routes and <code>ctx.api.databases.records</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The deprecated <code>primitive databases permissions grant</code> and <code>revoke</code> commands and the JavaScript client&#39;s <code>databases.grantPermission</code> and <code>revokePermission</code> are removed; use <code>primitive databases permissions add-manager</code> / <code>remove-manager</code>, or <code>ctx.api.databases.addManager</code> / <code>revokePermission</code> from a server function.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The JavaScript client removes <code>FunctionWaitForResult</code>, <code>documents.createOffline</code>, <code>waitForSync</code> and <code>updateLocalSnapshotFlag</code> (use <code>FunctionRunSettled</code>, <code>documents.create</code> and <code>waitForInitialSync</code>), and both clients drop <code>user_created_at_epoch_s</code> from analytics event input.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> In the Swift client, an HTTP call made while the client is offline throws <code>JsBaoError</code> with code <code>.offline</code> without sending the request, as the JavaScript client does, so code that detects a missing network by catching <code>JsBaoNetworkError</code> must also catch <code>.offline</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A retention policy&#39;s <code>maxDocs</code> and TTL (<code>ttlMs</code>, Swift <code>ttl</code>) must be positive in both clients: <code>0</code>, which the JavaScript client read as no limit and the Swift client as evict everything, is refused <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code>, so omit the field (Swift <code>nil</code>) for no limit.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> On production, experimental surfaces are not served — the notification inbox and sending, the <code>notification</code> event, resource metadata and its categories, and rule-set debug answer 404 to the clients and are refused to server functions, while every other environment serves them.</li><li>Both clients report connected once the server can deliver the user&#39;s events to them — notifications, document metadata, agent sessions and access requests — so a list made on connect and the events that follow miss nothing; a connection that never gets there within the sync handshake timeout is retried.</li><li>In a browser tab, the JavaScript client keeps its connection id across reloads, and each tab has its own, so a reload reconnects about a second sooner and a connection id your app stored still reaches the same tab.</li><li>Every public method of the JavaScript and Swift clients states its stability — production, beta or experimental — in its doc comment, and deprecated ones are struck through in editors or warn at compile time with their successor.</li><li>A request to a known path with a method it does not accept answers 405 <code>METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED</code> with an <code>Allow</code> header, on the app and admin APIs.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> In the JavaScript client, <code>client.ruleSets</code> (author rule sets in <code>rule-sets/</code> and <code>config push</code>) and <code>notifications.send</code> (send from a server function), and in both clients <code>collections.listAll</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A database record saved without an <code>id</code> is given one, as documented, instead of being refused.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A WebSocket connection opened with an access token is closed when the token is revoked, deleted or expires, instead of staying open.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The JavaScript client bypasses the browser&#39;s HTTP cache on every request, so concurrent reads of the same resource no longer wait on one another.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A WebSocket connection opened with an access token, or reopened right after a disconnect, is no longer closed with <code>4001 SESSION_INVALID</code> the next time it sends after a quiet period.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In the JavaScript client, going back online soon after <code>goOffline</code> keeps the new connection, instead of dropping it and resetting every open document when the old connection&#39;s close arrives late.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In the Swift client, queued blob uploads survive a process restart and are sent by the next client for the same app and user, as the JavaScript client&#39;s do; an upload queued by one user is never attempted, listed or cancelled while another user is signed in.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In Node.js, a <code>better-sqlite3</code> that is installed but not built fails sign-in with <code>STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE</code> naming the fix, instead of signing in without a session store; a client that is not installed still falls back to the in-memory store, and <code>storageConfig: { type: &quot;memory&quot; }</code> chooses it outright.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A browser build of an app using the JavaScript client no longer bundles <code>better-sqlite3</code> or warns about externalized Node built-ins or a missing <code>y-indexeddb</code> <code>default</code> export, and the Vue template&#39;s build prints none of those warnings.</li></ul><h3 id="configuration-and-cli" tabindex="-1">Configuration and CLI <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration-and-cli" aria-label="Permalink to “Configuration and CLI”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Scoped CLI logins and step-ups.</strong> <code>primitive login --scope &lt;list&gt; [--app &lt;ids&gt;]</code> asks for a session limited to those scopes and pinned to those apps, granted only after you approve it on a console page that names them. <code>primitive login --step-up --scope &lt;list&gt;</code> asks for a 15-minute step-up holding exactly those scopes, held beside your login and pinned to the environment&#39;s app unless <code>--app</code> names others; a list with <code>admin</code> is a step-up without the flag, and only then does Google ask you to sign in again. A command refused for lacking <code>admin</code>, or by a protected app, retries once with the step-up, and a <code>config push --prune</code> that would hard-delete a function, integration or prompt stops before applying anything without it, naming them; <code>primitive logout</code> ends it too. <code>primitive token --scope &lt;list&gt; [--app &lt;ids&gt;] [--ttl &lt;d&gt;]</code> prints a narrower token for a script or agent, with no browser unless the request is wider than your login; <code>primitive token --step-up --scope &lt;list&gt;</code> prints a step-up, always after approval. <code>primitive whoami</code> shows the session&#39;s kind, scope and pinned apps and any step-up, and <code>primitive auth sessions list</code> shows <code>step-up</code> sessions and <code>pending</code> approvals.</li><li><strong>CI sessions.</strong> <code>primitive auth sessions create --name &lt;name&gt; --scope &lt;list&gt; --app &lt;ids&gt; [--ttl &lt;d&gt;]</code> creates a named admin token for a CI job once you approve it in the browser, and prints it once. It is pinned to its apps, never holds <code>admin</code>, and lives 90 days unless <code>--ttl</code> says otherwise, at most 1 year. Run the CLI with it in <code>PRIMITIVE_TOKEN</code>, with no credentials file; it works on the admin and app APIs and WebSocket connections within its scope and pins. <code>primitive auth sessions list</code> shows it with its name, and once revoked its token fails on its next request.</li><li><strong>Protection levels.</strong> <code>protection</code> under <code>[app]</code> in <code>app.toml</code> sets an app&#39;s protection level: <code>default</code>, <code>protected</code> for an app with real users, or <code>internal</code> for a throwaway app. A new app is <code>default</code>, and a child app is <code>internal</code> and cannot be changed (<code>400 PROTECTION_FIXED_ON_CHILD</code>). <code>config push</code> applies it, and <code>primitive apps get</code>, <code>primitive apps children get</code>, <code>GET /settings</code>, the admin app object and the web-admin dashboard and settings page show it. Only the app&#39;s console owner can change it: anyone else&#39;s change — a console admin&#39;s, or any app-user token&#39;s — is refused with <code>403 PROTECTION_OWNER_ONLY</code> and nothing is applied, while a body carrying the current level saves as before. A value that is not a level is a <code>400</code>. A pulled <code>app.toml</code> always states the level; a file without the line pushes <code>default</code>.</li><li>Deleting an <code>internal</code> app, child apps included, needs <code>write</code> instead of an <code>admin</code> step-up, and a login pinned to an app creates that app&#39;s child apps and lists them with it; a <code>protected</code> parent asks for no step-up for the create.</li><li><code>PATCH /admin/api/me</code> with <code>{ &quot;name&quot; }</code> changes your Admin Console display name.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> <code>protected = true</code> under <code>[app]</code> reads as <code>protection = &quot;protected&quot;</code> and <code>protected = false</code> as not protected, until the first breaking release after 2026-12-15; a push of a file carrying it prints a warning, and <code>primitive config pull --only app</code> rewrites it as <code>protection</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> On a <code>protected</code> app, a CLI login&#39;s writes — anything beyond <code>read</code> and <code>data:read</code>, on the admin API, the app API and WebSocket connections — need a step-up pinned to the app, even from a login pinned to it, and are refused with <code>403 PROTECTED_APP_STEP_UP_REQUIRED</code> naming <code>primitive login --step-up --scope &lt;scopes&gt; --app &lt;id&gt;</code>; a WebSocket connection from such a login reads only, and a write over it gets that code and command. A pinned CI session writes with no step-up, and a <code>default</code> or <code>internal</code> app, the <code>internal</code> child of a protected app included, never needs one. Lowering an app&#39;s level from <code>protected</code> needs <code>admin</code>. A step-up holds exactly the scopes it was approved for, <code>admin</code> only when asked for, and a refusal naming <code>admin</code> prints a step-up sized to the route.</li><li><strong>Root documents as large documents.</strong> <code>rootDocumentFormat = 3</code> under <code>[app]</code> in <code>app.toml</code> creates each new user&#39;s root document as a large document; omit it, or set <code>1</code>, for an ordinary root. Existing root documents keep their format. <code>GET /settings</code> and the admin app object report it, and a value other than <code>1</code>, <code>3</code> or empty is refused with <code>400 INVALID_ROOT_DOCUMENT_FORMAT</code>, and a child app copies it from its parent. <code>primitive documents import</code> refuses to merge an ordinary root&#39;s export into a large root document.</li><li>A project holds one login per server: every environment on that server, a linked git worktree and a child app included, uses it, <code>login</code> and <code>logout</code> in any of them act on all of them and say which, and a stored login is sent only to the server that issued it, so changing an environment&#39;s <code>apiUrl</code> needs a new <code>primitive login</code>.</li><li>A web app built with Vite resolves the project&#39;s environment the way the CLI does through the <code>primitiveEnv()</code> plugin that <code>primitive-admin/vite</code> exports, which fills <code>VITE_APP_ID</code>, <code>VITE_API_URL</code> and <code>VITE_WS_URL</code> from <code>primitive/config.json</code> for the app and its tests.</li><li><code>primitive databases import --json</code> reports, for each database, the id it was written to in the target app.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Every <code>primitive login</code> is approved on a console page that names the apps it reaches, what it can do and how long it lasts; nothing is granted until you press <strong>Approve</strong>. Inside a project a plain login is pinned to the project&#39;s apps on that server and their child apps, and a command on any other app is refused with <code>APP_NOT_PINNED</code> naming the login that covers it; <code>primitive login --app all</code> asks for every app you administer, which a login outside a project must do (<code>--app all</code> replaces <code>--app none</code>), and <code>login</code> prints the scope, the apps by name and the environments it signed in to. A plain login holds the default scope <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>data:read</code> and <code>data:write</code>, so deleting a <code>default</code> or <code>protected</code> app or a database, removing a collaborator or changing a user&#39;s role needs a step-up (<code>primitive login --step-up --scope admin</code>); the CLI retries once with it. A CLI login made before scoped sessions is refused at its next refresh and tells you to run <code>primitive login</code>. Console sessions refresh only through their cookie.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Database type configs name their rule set with <code>ruleSetName</code> in TOML; <code>config push</code> refuses a <code>ruleSetId</code> line there.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Inside a project, <code>models/models.toml</code> (or <code>src/models/models.toml</code>) is the only document schema <code>config push</code>, <code>config pull</code>, <code>config diff</code> and <code>primitive functions codegen</code> read. They no longer write <code>functions/primitive-document-schema.generated.toml</code> there, and remove a leftover one with a line saying so; with no schema file, <code>functions/primitive-document-types.d.ts</code> is not written and a leftover one is removed. A project that deleted <code>models/models.toml</code> but whose running functions carry a schema reads Modified in <code>config diff</code>, and its next push drops the schema with a warning: to keep it, restore <code>models/models.toml</code> from version control before pushing. A tree outside any project still falls back to the generated file.</li><li>Outside a project, <code>primitive apps delete &lt;app-id&gt;</code> deletes an app through the login for the default server or the one <code>PRIMITIVE_SERVER_URL</code> names, with its step-up and <code>--with-children</code>, and <code>primitive whoami</code> reports the user, that server and where it came from, the login or <code>PRIMITIVE_TOKEN</code> in use and no app — or says you are not logged in and names the server to sign in to.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A blob bucket&#39;s <code>accessPolicy</code> is removed from bucket TOML, the bucket API and both clients and is refused with <code>400 RETIRED_REQUEST_KEY</code>, so set <code>preset</code> (<code>public</code>, <code>authenticated</code> or <code>admin-only</code>) instead.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A prompt or integration test case names its configuration and judge prompt only by <code>configName</code>, <code>evaluatorPromptKey</code> and <code>evaluatorConfigName</code>, and <code>config push</code> refuses a case file that carries <code>configId</code>, <code>evaluatorPromptId</code> or <code>evaluatorConfigId</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>config push</code> refuses <code>ruleSetId</code> in a blob bucket&#39;s TOML; name the rule set with <code>ruleSetName</code>, which push resolves in the app you push to, and <code>config pull</code> rewrites a file that still carries the id.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>primitive catalog</code> and <code>primitive feature-flags</code> are removed from the CLI.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>primitive workflows</code> and <code>primitive scripts</code>, which only read workflows and scripts an app stored before server functions, are removed, along with the local <code>config migrate-toml</code> and <code>config migrate-v2</code> verbs. That history stays readable through the admin API (<code>GET /admin/api/apps/{appId}/workflows</code> and <code>GET /admin/api/apps/{appId}/scripts</code>), and <code>config push</code>, <code>config pull</code> and <code>config diff</code> are unchanged.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The production CLI has no experimental commands: <code>primitive metadata</code> (except <code>metadata report</code>), <code>primitive metadata-category-configs</code> and <code>primitive rule-sets debug</code> are gone from it, and <code>config pull</code>, <code>config diff</code> and <code>config push</code> leave a <code>metadata-category-configs/</code> directory alone — no listing, push, prune, validation or comparison. To keep using them outside production, install the alpha build: <code>npm install -g primitive-admin@alpha</code>.</li><li><code>primitive whoami</code> shows your app-creation limit, <code>primitive apps get</code> shows a child app&#39;s lineage, <code>primitive email-templates preview &lt;type&gt;</code> renders a template with sample variables without sending it, and <code>primitive secrets set --clear-summary</code> removes a secret&#39;s description.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> <code>GET</code> and <code>PUT /admin/api/profile</code>; read and rename your account with <code>GET</code> and <code>PATCH /admin/api/me</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>config pull</code> leaves a file untouched when <code>config diff</code> reports it in sync, so its comments, key order and explicit defaults survive the pull. Files that differ from the server are still rewritten, and the pull summary reports how many files were written and how many were left unchanged.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive documents export</code> and <code>export-all</code> no longer report success when a document&#39;s permissions, pending invitations, aliases or blob list can&#39;t be read. A 404 still means &quot;none&quot;; a transient failure is retried, and any other failure names the document and exits non-zero. <code>export-all</code> exports the remaining documents and lists the failed ones. A document with more than one page of blobs now exports all of them, not just the first page.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive documents import</code> retries a transient failure, reports each document that failed as failed or incomplete and continues with the rest, exiting non-zero at the end; re-running it completes a document a previous run left without its state or some of its blobs, large documents included, and skips those already whole.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive databases import</code> restores an export&#39;s indexes and unique constraints instead of failing on its <code>indexes.json</code> after creating the database, and checks every file of the export before it creates anything.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive users set-role &lt;user-id&gt; &lt;role&gt;</code> works in its documented two-argument form instead of exiting with a missing-argument error.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>config push</code> of an <code>[invitations]</code> or <code>largeDocumentWindowDays</code> edit no longer leaves <code>config diff --only app</code> reporting drift, because the settings write answers with every field a read returns.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>config push --prune</code> deletes an object archived since the last sync and fails the push on a delete the server refuses, instead of planning the delete, archiving the object and then reporting it as modified on the server.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive users invite &lt;email&gt;</code> invites into the current app without an app id, instead of exiting with a missing-argument error.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive users invitations list</code> shows every invitation with a <code>STATUS</code> column (pending, accepted or expired), instead of listing accepted ones as pending, and <code>primitive users invitations revoke &lt;invitation-id&gt;</code> withdraws one.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>primitive users transfer-owner &lt;email&gt;</code> transfers the current app&#39;s ownership without an app id, instead of reading the email as the app id.</li></ul><h3 id="starter-templates" tabindex="-1">Starter templates <a class="header-anchor" href="#starter-templates" aria-label="Permalink to “Starter templates”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The <code>primitive-app</code> (npm) and <code>swift-primitive-app</code> (SwiftPM products <code>PrimitiveApp</code> and <code>PrimitiveAppTesting</code>) app libraries are retired. Nothing is published after <code>primitive-app</code> <code>3.1.3</code> (2026-09-30) and the <code>swift-primitive-app</code> mirror&#39;s <code>main</code> branch at <code>4be1bf1</code> (2026-09-30); the alpha channel stops at <code>primitive-app</code> <code>3.2.0-alpha.26</code> and the mirror&#39;s <code>alpha</code> branch at <code>a0d3c1a</code> (2026-10-28). Published versions stay available, and <code>primitive-app</code> is marked deprecated on npm after this release. The frozen <code>swift-primitive-app</code> depends on <code>swift-client</code> by branch, so a later <code>swift package update</code> can stop it building: migrate rather than pinning. The current starter templates carry everything the libraries provided as project code (<code>primitive init</code> scaffolds them; the <code>primitive-platform</code> skill&#39;s upgrade workflow, Step 6, applies them to an existing app). The migration replaces the libraries&#39; starter pieces only: an app&#39;s own data model, root-document access, prefs, theme and group code stay the app&#39;s. <ul><li>Web: replace <code>initializeJsBao</code> / <code>jsBaoClientService</code> with the template&#39;s <code>src/lib/client.ts</code> (<code>initializeAppClient</code>, <code>getAppClient</code>); take its <code>userStore</code>, <code>PrimitiveLogin</code>, <code>PrimitiveSignInCallback</code> and onboarding, which use the client&#39;s <code>emailSignInLinkTarget()</code> and <code>handleSignInCallback()</code>; import <code>primitiveEnv</code> from <code>primitive-admin/vite</code> instead of <code>primitive-app/vite</code> (<code>pnpm add -D primitive-admin@latest</code>); remove <code>primitiveDevTools</code> and its routes; move <code>*.primitive-test.ts</code> groups to plain vitest files signed in with <code>src/tests/support/signIn.ts</code>; take <code>useJsBaoDataLoader</code> (options <code>ready</code> and <code>reloadOnDb3Events</code>); then <code>pnpm remove primitive-app</code>. User prefs (<code>getPref</code> and friends), theming, <code>isMemberOf</code> / <code>memberGroups</code> and the root document the library opened have no template replacement: an app that uses them keeps its own copy of that code, and its prefs data stays in the root document.</li><li>iOS: replace <code>PrimitiveAppState</code> and <code>PrimitiveAuthManager</code> with the template&#39;s <code>AppState</code> and <code>AuthModel</code> (with <code>AuthGate</code>, <code>LoginView</code>, <code>OnboardingView</code>, <code>ProfileView</code>), which call <code>client.auth.emailSignInRequest(email:)</code> and <code>client.auth.handleSignInCallback(_:)</code>; opt into custom-scheme sign-in links with the <code>PrimitiveOAuthRedirectURI</code> Info.plist key in place of <code>sendsEmailSignInLink</code>; take <code>BaoDataLoader</code> (reloads on <code>db3:ready</code>, <code>db3:gone</code>, <code>db3:evicted</code>); move <code>InspectorTest</code> groups to XCTest in the template&#39;s test target with <code>TestSignIn.client()</code> and <code>./test.sh</code>; remove the Debug Inspector and its Bonjour service. Keep what is the app&#39;s: its <code>models.toml</code> schema stays <code>bao-codegen.json</code>&#39;s <code>input</code> (the template ships <code>{ &quot;input&quot;: null }</code> only because a fresh scaffold has no model), code that opens the root document or reads <code>ThemeManager</code> moves into the app before the dependency goes (the library&#39;s copies are no longer published; the template provides neither). Then drop the <code>PrimitiveApp</code> and <code>PrimitiveAppTesting</code> products from <code>Package.swift</code> and <code>project.yml</code>, keeping <code>JsBaoClient</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>emailSignInRequest</code> with no <code>redirectUri</code> and no <code>oauthRedirectUri</code> set links to the page&#39;s origin plus <code>/oauth/callback</code> by default, where it used to send a code-only email: add that URL to the app&#39;s <code>[auth].emailRedirectUris</code> and run <code>config push</code>, or pass <code>redirectUri: null</code> to keep asking for a code only. A call that already passes a <code>redirectUri</code>, or a client with <code>oauthRedirectUri</code> set, is unaffected.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The Vue template&#39;s <code>pnpm cf-deploy</code> refuses a bare environment name; name both environments with <code>--deploy-env</code> and <code>--primitive-env</code>.</li><li>A fresh <code>primitive init</code> scaffold, web or iOS, ships its sign-in, onboarding and client setup as project code over the client packages alone (<code>js-bao</code>, <code>js-bao-wss-client</code> and <code>primitive-admin</code> on the web, <code>JsBaoClient</code> on iOS): <code>pnpm codegen</code> and <code>scripts/codegen.sh</code> generate models from the environment&#39;s <code>db3-types/</code>, <code>pnpm test</code> and <code>./test.sh</code> run the project&#39;s own tests against the app, and the dev tools overlay, in-browser test harness, user prefs, theming and the Debug Inspector are no longer included.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The web template&#39;s <code>codegen</code> copies the installed sql.js&#39;s <code>sql-wasm.wasm</code> into <code>public/</code>, so a fresh install that resolves a newer sql.js no longer hangs at <code>dbReady</code>, and a JS/wasm version mismatch is reported instead of hanging.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> In the Swift app layer, signing in after a sign-out in the same process no longer shows a false &quot;could not connect&quot; error or keeps the previous account&#39;s profile and documents.</li></ul><h2 id="_2026-09-30" tabindex="-1">2026-09-30 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-09-30" aria-label="Permalink to “2026-09-30”">​</a></h2><h3 id="server-functions-1" tabindex="-1">Server functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#server-functions-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Server functions”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Server functions are TypeScript you keep in your config tree, ship with <code>primitive config push</code>, and call from the JavaScript client, the Swift client or the CLI with <code>functions.invoke</code>.</li><li>Any function can also run as a task with <code>functions.start</code>, pausing with <code>step.sleep</code> for hours or days and tracked with <code>getStatus</code> and <code>waitFor</code>.</li><li>A function works on the app&#39;s authority through <code>ctx</code>: typed database and document handles, prompts, integrations, secrets, config vars, the app&#39;s users, and starting other functions.</li><li><code>ctx.channels</code> authorizes and publishes to realtime channels that both clients join with <code>subscribeToChannel</code>, and <code>ctx.users.send</code> delivers direct messages to a user&#39;s live connections.</li><li>A function can declare its own webhook and cron triggers, and a workflow&#39;s <code>workflow.call</code> step can call a function by key.</li><li><code>primitive functions codegen</code> generates typed invokers for TypeScript and Swift, and <code>config push</code> typechecks every function before shipping it.</li><li>Every invocation leaves a seven-day log (<code>primitive functions logs</code>), and a failed run carries a typed <code>errorCode</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="large-documents-1" tabindex="-1">Large documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#large-documents-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Large documents”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A document created with <code>documentFormat: 2</code> (CLI: <code>primitive documents create --large</code>) holds up to 2 GB of records and opens through the same document, model and query APIs in the JavaScript and Swift clients.</li><li>Clients keep a local copy that syncs incrementally, can load only some models on a constrained device, and query or aggregate across all of a user&#39;s open large documents in one call.</li><li>Offline writes are reconciled on reconnect and reported through <code>documentOfflineWritesResolved</code>, and a client offline longer than <code>largeDocumentWindowDays</code> (default 7) is read-only until it syncs.</li><li><code>primitive documents ingest</code> and <code>client.documents.ingests</code> bulk-load records from NDJSON files, and connected clients pick up the load without reloading.</li><li><code>primitive documents snapshots</code> lists a document&#39;s snapshot builds and builds one on demand.</li></ul><h3 id="documents-1" tabindex="-1">Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#documents-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Documents”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>validateAccess</code> takes a <code>userId</code> to answer what another user may do with a document.</li><li><code>createWithAlias</code> and <code>getOrCreateWithAlias</code> take <code>documentFormat</code>, <code>tags</code> and <code>metadata</code>.</li><li>A client that expects the wrong format for a document is refused with <code>DOCUMENT_FORMAT_MISMATCH</code>, and records requests may state the format they expect.</li><li><code>client.registerModel</code> adds a model to a running JavaScript client.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> JavaScript client 3.0.0 removes <code>documents.list()</code> and per-document invitations, replaced by <code>me.ownedDocuments()</code>, <code>me.sharedDocuments()</code> and sharing by email with <code>documents.updatePermissions</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The Swift client&#39;s <code>documents.create</code> no longer opens the new document, so open it before writing.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Document create routes refuse body keys they do not read with <code>400 VALIDATION_FAILED</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A document whose sync stalls reports <code>documentSyncStateChanged</code> with <code>state: &quot;error&quot;</code>, and the client reconnects on its own.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> An awaited <code>documents.open()</code> in the JavaScript client resolves only once the synced records are queryable.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Records queries handle <code>$or</code> with many branches, sorting on a field some records lack, and a unique key whose earlier record was deleted.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A records request to a document that is being deleted answers <code>404</code> instead of <code>500</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Large updates sent inline, as the Swift client sends them, are stored durably.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The Swift client handles numbers at or above 2^63, evicts <code>localOnly</code> and revoked documents correctly, and sends a <code>transactAndSync</code> transaction once.</li></ul><h3 id="models-and-queries-1" tabindex="-1">Models and queries <a class="header-anchor" href="#models-and-queries-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Models and queries”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>$ne</code> and <code>$nin</code> match records that never wrote the field, so <code>{ deleted: { $ne: true } }</code> means &quot;false or not set&quot;.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A unique constraint on a <code>stringset</code> field is refused where it is declared (js-bao 0.11.0, codegen and <code>config push</code>), so remove it and regenerate.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Model fields named <code>type</code> or starting with <code>_</code> are refused at <code>config push</code> and codegen.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> A grouped <code>Model.aggregate</code> with a single <code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code> or <code>max</code> returns the bare value per group (js-bao 0.10.0).</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Each <code>initJsBao</code> call creates its own isolated instance, and <code>js-bao-wss-client</code> requires <code>js-bao</code> 0.6.0 or newer.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Database <code>save</code> keeps a field written as <code>null</code>, and a unique constraint no longer fires for an index entry left by a deleted record.</li></ul><h3 id="api-and-clients-1" tabindex="-1">API and clients <a class="header-anchor" href="#api-and-clients-1" aria-label="Permalink to “API and clients”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Every API error response is JSON with a machine-readable <code>code</code>, exposed as <code>JsBaoApiError.code</code>, <code>HttpError.serverCode</code> in Swift and <code>ApiError.code</code> in the CLI (see the Error Handling guide).</li><li>App API responses are <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>, and the Swift client never caches HTTP responses.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Error bodies that were plain text are now JSON, so code calling the API with a raw <code>fetch</code> reads <code>body.error</code> instead of the text.</li><li><strong>Deprecated:</strong> The CEL context surfaces, the <code>cursor</code> alias of <code>nextCursor</code>, and removing a user (use <code>users disable</code>) are marked deprecated on every surface.</li></ul><h3 id="prompts-1" tabindex="-1">Prompts <a class="header-anchor" href="#prompts-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Prompts”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A <code>kind = &quot;decisions&quot;</code> prompt answers typed questions about a state with probabilities, with each run able to supply its own options.</li><li><code>strictOutput = true</code> on a chat config has the provider enforce the prompt&#39;s <code>outputSchema</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The direct <code>llm</code> and <code>gemini</code> APIs are removed from both clients, server functions and <code>app.toml</code>, so run a prompt with <code>ctx.prompts.run</code> instead.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A failed run reports the provider&#39;s <code>upstreamStatus</code> and <code>PROMPT_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT</code>, and a template&#39;s trailing newline survives <code>config push</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="collections-1" tabindex="-1">Collections <a class="header-anchor" href="#collections-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Collections”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>primitive collections export</code> and <code>import</code> move collections with their owners, members and grants between apps.</li><li><code>primitive collections create --owner</code> creates a collection on behalf of a user, and <code>listCollectionsForDocument</code> pages.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Collection group grants are recomputed on every change, and access listings return every entry instead of the first 100.</li></ul><h3 id="locks-1" tabindex="-1">Locks <a class="header-anchor" href="#locks-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Locks”">​</a></h3><ul><li>An <code>owner</code> passed when acquiring a lock lets that owner re-take its own lease.</li></ul><h3 id="sign-in-and-sessions-1" tabindex="-1">Sign-in and sessions <a class="header-anchor" href="#sign-in-and-sessions-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Sign-in and sessions”">​</a></h3><ul><li>The <code>passkeyUserVerification</code> app setting (<code>preferred</code> or <code>required</code>) sets one verification policy for both passkey ceremonies.</li><li>Every admin sign-in is a revocable session (<code>primitive auth sessions list</code> and <code>revoke</code>), and <code>primitive logout</code> ends it on the server.</li><li>Environments accept an <code>iosAppId</code>, and <code>pnpm cf-deploy</code> generates the matching <code>apple-app-site-association</code> file.</li><li><code>PrimitiveAuthManager.authFailure</code> in the Swift app layer exposes the typed code of the last failed sign-in.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Passkey sign-in from iOS respects preferred verification, and the Swift sign-in flow forwards <code>rpId</code> and reports the server&#39;s error codes.</li></ul><h3 id="analytics-1" tabindex="-1">Analytics <a class="header-anchor" href="#analytics-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Analytics”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>users.search</code> filters by signup day, and prompt analytics report per-prompt cost.</li><li>Server-function failures are grouped in error analytics by function key.</li><li>Cron and system workflow runs no longer count as user activity.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>users.top</code> reports each user&#39;s all-time <code>firstSeen</code>, so a returning user no longer reads as new.</li></ul><h3 id="workflows-webhooks-and-integrations" tabindex="-1">Workflows, webhooks and integrations <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-webhooks-and-integrations" aria-label="Permalink to “Workflows, webhooks and integrations”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>primitive analytics workflow-usage</code> reports which step kinds an app&#39;s workflows use and how often they ran.</li><li>Workflows, server functions, scripts and webhooks share one key namespace, enforced at <code>config push</code>.</li><li><code>primitive webhooks test --deliver</code> sends the signed preview to the real endpoint so the handler actually runs.</li><li><code>primitive integrations logs --run</code> shows the calls a server-function run made.</li><li>The Rhai script step&#39;s operation ceiling is 250,000.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Model-typed operation parameters are coerced like workflow input, a <code>switch</code> step&#39;s outputs resolve, and creates and starts no longer fail on a delayed read-back.</li></ul><h3 id="cli" tabindex="-1">CLI <a class="header-anchor" href="#cli" aria-label="Permalink to “CLI”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The configuration tree lives in <code>primitive/&lt;env&gt;/</code> beside <code>primitive/config.json</code>, <code>--env</code> selects another environment, and <code>--dir</code>, <code>--sync-dir</code>, <code>primitive use</code> and <code>primitive context</code> are removed.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Every <code>list</code> prints one page, and <code>--json</code> prints the <code>{ items, hasMore, nextCursor }</code> envelope.</li><li><code>config push</code> validates the whole tree before applying anything and reports a var changed only on the server as drift.</li><li>Destructive collection and membership verbs take <code>--json</code>, and admin document listings show tags.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>config diff</code> no longer reports identical types as modified, test verbs accept keys, and <code>documents export</code> and <code>import</code> keep tags and root documents.</li></ul><h3 id="admin-api" tabindex="-1">Admin API <a class="header-anchor" href="#admin-api" aria-label="Permalink to “Admin API”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A user&#39;s document and database inventories page with <code>{ items, hasMore, nextCursor }</code> and reach every item.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> List your apps with <code>GET /admin/api/admins/me/apps</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="starter-templates-1" tabindex="-1">Starter templates <a class="header-anchor" href="#starter-templates-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Starter templates”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>pnpm lint</code> in the Vue template reports uses of deprecated platform APIs.</li><li>Generated sources are committed and regenerated on every build.</li><li>The Swift template&#39;s App Store lanes sign entirely from the App Store Connect API key.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A new Swift app passes App Store validation, archives on the first try, and builds under Xcode 27 and SwiftBuild.</li></ul><h2 id="_2026-08-28" tabindex="-1">2026-08-28 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-08-28" aria-label="Permalink to “2026-08-28”">​</a></h2><h3 id="sign-in" tabindex="-1">Sign-in <a class="header-anchor" href="#sign-in" aria-label="Permalink to “Sign-in”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Passkey calls can name their relying party (<code>rpId</code> in JavaScript, <code>AuthConfig(passkeyRpId:)</code> in Swift).</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A passkey request naming an unconfigured relying party is refused with <code>PASSKEY_RP_NOT_CONFIGURED</code>, and a failed OAuth exchange throws a typed error in JavaScript.</li></ul><h3 id="documents-2" tabindex="-1">Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#documents-2" aria-label="Permalink to “Documents”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Shares granted by email before the recipient signs up resolve reliably at signup.</li></ul><h3 id="cli-and-templates" tabindex="-1">CLI and templates <a class="header-anchor" href="#cli-and-templates" aria-label="Permalink to “CLI and templates”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Environments accept a <code>webUrl</code>, and <code>primitive init</code> scaffolds universal-link sign-in for an app with both iOS and web clients.</li><li>The Swift template&#39;s <code>run-ios.sh</code> boots a simulator dedicated to the app.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>integrations tests</code> resolves by key or ID, <code>scripts tests</code> names the cases it skips, and the Swift template finds its App Store Connect key from any directory.</li></ul><h2 id="_2026-08-27" tabindex="-1">2026-08-27 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-08-27" aria-label="Permalink to “2026-08-27”">​</a></h2><h3 id="sign-in-1" tabindex="-1">Sign-in <a class="header-anchor" href="#sign-in-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Sign-in”">​</a></h3><ul><li>An app with both iOS and web clients emails one sign-in link that opens the iOS app where it is installed and the web app everywhere else.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Email sign-in sends one email carrying both a code and a link, controlled by <code>[auth].emailSignInEnabled</code>, and the <code>magic-link</code> and <code>otp</code> templates are retired in favor of <code>email-sign-in</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Google sign-in is configured per client type under <code>[auth.google.clients.&lt;type&gt;]</code>, <code>redirectUris</code> becomes <code>emailRedirectUris</code>, and older clients hide Google sign-in until upgraded.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Swift client sessions restore on cold start, refresh reliably, and clear fully on sign-out.</li></ul><h3 id="access-and-credentials" tabindex="-1">Access and credentials <a class="header-anchor" href="#access-and-credentials" aria-label="Permalink to “Access and credentials”">​</a></h3><ul><li>An app can install a <code>lock</code> rule set controlling which keys members may acquire.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Prompts, workflows and integrations require an <code>accessRule</code>, and a resource without one refuses callers.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Webhook signing secrets and Google client secrets accept only secret references, and app secrets are the only credential store.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> The direct LLM and Gemini proxy routes are off unless <code>directLlmEnabled = true</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="workflows" tabindex="-1">Workflows <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows" aria-label="Permalink to “Workflows”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Templates can read whether an upstream step succeeded, failed or was skipped.</li><li>The database write step accepts several records per call, and the iterate-users step takes a <code>metadataFilter</code>.</li><li><code>forEach</code> iterates up to 500 items by default and fails, naming the limit, instead of truncating.</li><li>Declarative locks apply to synchronous runs and <code>workflow.call</code>, and guards read every app config var through <code>vars.KEY</code>.</li><li>Multi-step runs keep sub-second step gaps wherever they run.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> An unresolved template reference fails the step, and <code>config push</code> validates <code>{{ }}</code> expressions.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>prompt.execute</code> parses JSON only with <code>expect = &quot;json&quot;</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Workflow push sends the full definition, so a field omitted from TOML is cleared.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Workflow document writes enforce the model&#39;s field types.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Runs are visible while in flight, <code>forEach</code> and template fallbacks behave, status is consistent across clients, and database reads return real booleans.</li></ul><h3 id="configuration-and-cli-1" tabindex="-1">Configuration and CLI <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration-and-cli-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Configuration and CLI”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>primitive &lt;noun&gt; archive</code> retires an integration, webhook, cron trigger, workflow or prompt, and deleting a workflow or prompt now archives it.</li><li><code>primitive documents</code> gains inspection, permission, create and delete verbs, and <code>databases records</code> gains get, count, aggregate, save and patch.</li><li>Log commands follow new entries with <code>--watch</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Server-side configuration is authored in TOML only, and the CLI&#39;s config-setting flags are removed.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> CLI commands are reorganized, with <code>sync</code> under <code>config</code>, <code>collections docs</code> renamed <code>documents</code>, <code>database-types</code> renamed <code>database-type-configs</code>, and the <code>llm</code> group removed.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>config push</code> applies <code>app.toml</code> as the complete state, so run <code>config pull --only app</code> first or omitted settings are cleared.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Availability status is no longer authored in TOML, so use <code>primitive &lt;noun&gt; enable</code> or <code>disable</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> <code>config diff</code> and <code>config push</code> compare every resource by content and agree, and a quoted number where a number is declared is now an error.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> <code>config push</code> adopts existing server resources by key, lands a multi-config prompt on the first push, and round-trips cleanly with <code>config pull</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="documents-and-databases" tabindex="-1">Documents and databases <a class="header-anchor" href="#documents-and-databases" aria-label="Permalink to “Documents and databases”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A resource can be found by a unique metadata value from both clients and the CLI.</li><li>Documents gain a records <code>aggregate</code> endpoint matching the databases one.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Admin data routes move under role-aware <code>records/*</code>, <code>databases.list</code> requires a filter, and bulk operations take <code>data</code> instead of <code>fields</code>.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Document permission entries type <code>name</code> as optional.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Long-lived documents reopen quickly, <code>localOnly</code> edits stay local, and documents deleted on the server are evicted from the local cache.</li></ul><h3 id="webhooks" tabindex="-1">Webhooks <a class="header-anchor" href="#webhooks" aria-label="Permalink to “Webhooks”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Webhooks can verify JWTs from a remote JWKS, custom detached signatures, or Plaid.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> An app is limited to 50 webhooks.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Webhook deduplication keys derive only from signed material, and JWT verification rejects a key id it cannot match.</li></ul><h3 id="swift-client" tabindex="-1">Swift client <a class="header-anchor" href="#swift-client" aria-label="Permalink to “Swift client”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Notifications, named locks and resource metadata match the JavaScript client.</li><li>Events are available as <code>AsyncStream</code>, <code>workflows.waitFor</code> survives disconnects, and codegen emits typed subscriptions and the full workflow factory.</li><li>Network monitoring, <code>waitForLoad</code>, <code>serverTimeoutMs</code> and cache-first document listing work as documented.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Deprecated symbols are removed in one batch, and the libraries build in Swift 6 language mode.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Connections, the document lifecycle and write ordering match the JavaScript client, and several crashes and decoding failures are gone.</li></ul><h3 id="javascript-client" tabindex="-1">JavaScript client <a class="header-anchor" href="#javascript-client" aria-label="Permalink to “JavaScript client”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>openDocument</code> errors carry typed codes.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A truncated listing no longer evicts documents, token refresh loops are capped, and switching accounts re-authenticates the connection.</li></ul><h3 id="analytics-and-admin-console" tabindex="-1">Analytics and admin console <a class="header-anchor" href="#analytics-and-admin-console" aria-label="Permalink to “Analytics and admin console”">​</a></h3><ul><li>The admin console groups recurring errors, shows live connections, and names the step a failed run failed on.</li><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> An analytics filter set over the cap is rejected instead of truncated.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Error groups no longer split on embedded ids or merge distinct failures, and adding a user by email is reliable.</li></ul><h2 id="_2026-07-24" tabindex="-1">2026-07-24 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-07-24" aria-label="Permalink to “2026-07-24”">​</a></h2><h3 id="documents-3" tabindex="-1">Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#documents-3" aria-label="Permalink to “Documents”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Single-document <code>save</code>, <code>patch</code> and <code>delete</code> accept <code>upsertOn</code>, <code>precondition</code> and <code>ifNotExists</code> for conditional writes.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> A just-created document appears in its creator&#39;s owned documents immediately.</li></ul><h3 id="sign-in-2" tabindex="-1">Sign-in <a class="header-anchor" href="#sign-in-2" aria-label="Permalink to “Sign-in”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A user can link several OAuth providers to one account.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Linking a second provider no longer locks the user out of the first.</li></ul><h3 id="analytics-and-workflows" tabindex="-1">Analytics and workflows <a class="header-anchor" href="#analytics-and-workflows" aria-label="Permalink to “Analytics and workflows”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Failures are recorded as fingerprinted error events, queryable with <code>errors.groups</code>.</li><li>A workflow fragment include accepts parameters.</li></ul><h3 id="lists-and-pagination" tabindex="-1">Lists and pagination <a class="header-anchor" href="#lists-and-pagination" aria-label="Permalink to “Lists and pagination”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Breaking:</strong> Every list endpoint returns <code>{ items, hasMore, nextCursor }</code>, and an invalid <code>limit</code> or cursor answers <code>400</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> The JavaScript client pages through every list result, and group memberships page fully.</li></ul><h3 id="cli-and-swift-client" tabindex="-1">CLI and Swift client <a class="header-anchor" href="#cli-and-swift-client" aria-label="Permalink to “CLI and Swift client”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>primitive blob-buckets head</code> reads a blob&#39;s metadata, and <code>primitive connections list</code> shows active connections.</li><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> Date-time values and <code>[workflow.lock]</code> survive <code>config push</code> and <code>pull</code>, and the Swift client&#39;s <code>waitForInSync</code> waits for real confirmation.</li></ul>',101)])])}const m=o(n,[["render",a]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
